   1: #!/usr/bin/env python3
   2: #
   3: #  #490: the DECLINE detector for dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c's two de-fatalised sites.
   4: #
   5: #      Site A   case 0x30, an unimplemented data format      (GDROM_W_FMT)
   6: #      Site B   default:, an unimplemented command byte      (GDROM_W_CMD)
   7: #
   8: #  Both were `fatal()` + `exit(1)` reached by an ordinary guest store, so a guest chose
   9: #  whether the emulator kept running.  Both now decline, announced ONCE per device instance
  10: #  behind their own latch bit, and `break` to the common tail.
  11: #
  12: #  *** WHY THIS IS A NEW PROBE RATHER THAN ROWS BOLTED ONTO gdrom_default_probe.py. ***
  13: #  #489's precedent, and it is about coupling rather than tidiness: gdrom_default's
  14: #  denominator is pinned as a literal in gate_sh_rounding.sh AND spelled out as a matching
  15: #  `for v in ...` name list, so every added row edits two places that must agree.  A separate
  16: #  probe with its own section and its own denominator has no such coupling.  Only the rows
  17: #  that must observe the EXISTING stale session stayed there.
  18: #
  19: #  *** THIS PARAGRAPH SAID "the 58-row probe" AND "a 58-name list" TWICE.  gdrom_default is
  20: #  now SIXTY -- this very round added `stale quiet` and `stale how` to it.  Corrected
  21: #  2026-08-29; the count is deliberately no longer written here at all. ***  Naming a
  22: #  sibling's row count in THIS file is the coupling the paragraph is arguing against, and it
  23: #  went stale in the same round that wrote it.
  24: #
  25: #  AND THE COUNT HAS NO SOUND STATIC DERIVER, which is why it must not be transcribed:
  26: #  `grep -c '^row('` on gdrom_default gives 52 and any-`row(` gives 55, because some rows are
  27: #  emitted from loops.  **The MEASURED `GDROM_DEF_RESULT=60/60` is the only authority.**  This
  28: #  probe is the easy case -- `^row(` = 20, agreeing with its measured 20/20 -- and that
  29: #  difference between two sibling files is exactly why a count needs its deriver named.
  30: #
  31: #  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  32: #  *** THE CONTRACT: WHAT THIS DETECTOR IS DESIGNED TO CATCH, so a later escape is either
  33: #  outside it or a breach of it rather than an argument about overclaiming. ***
  34: #
  35: #    1. reinstating either exit(1)                         -- `fmt surv` / `unk surv`
  36: #    2. the decline not announcing at all                  -- `fmt says` / `unk says`
  37: #    3. the announce losing its once-only latch            -- `fmt x2`  / `unk x2`
  38: #    4. the two sites sharing ONE latch bit                -- `both sites`
  39: #    5. either latch bit colliding WITH `GDROM_W_TOCLEN`    -- `crossbit`
  40: #    6. `break` becoming `return` (skipping the tail)      -- `fmt cnt` / `unk cnt`,
  41: #                                                             `fmt event` / `unk event`
  42: #    7. the `break` slipping INSIDE the latch `if`         -- `latch cond`
  43: #    8. the guard growing to swallow a legal format        -- `acceptA`
  44: #    9. the upstream 0x24 arm being folded into a decline  -- `fmt 0x24`
  45: #
  46: #  *** CLAIM 5 READ "COLLIDING WITH AN EXISTING BIT" AND THAT OVERCLAIMED BY A FACTOR OF
  47: #  SEVEN.  Narrowed 2026-08-29 after a pass-2 seat named the escape. ***  `crossbit` stages
  48: #  only a TOC decline, so it can see a collision with `GDROM_W_TOCLEN` and NOTHING ELSE.
  49: #  Aliasing either new bit onto any of the other six -- e.g.
  50: #  `#define GDROM_W_FMT (1u << GDROM_W_OVERREAD_B)` -- is PREDICTED to score 20/20, because
  51: #  no decline session performs an overread, a length mismatch or a DMA arm first, so the
  52: #  aliased bit is clear in every session and every latch behaves normally.
  53: #
  54: #  DELIBERATELY NOT CLOSED BY BUILDING TWELVE MORE ALIAS MUTANTS: that fails the addition
  55: #  licence's economics, and the enum-by-construction shape bounds the realistic edit to a
  56: #  hand-written raw mask.  `Ma_toclen`/`Mb_toclen` prove the most-benign-traffic instance IS
  57: #  caught.  The contract now claims what was measured and discloses the rest, which is what
  58: #  clause (e) is for.
  59: #
  60: #  *** THREE MEASURED 20/20 ESCAPES, BUILT BY THE PASS-2 ASK-AGAIN.  Disclosed 2026-08-29;
  61: #  artifacts/gdromexitsix/p2askagain_20260829/, every log sentinel-verified, each mutant's
  62: #  20-row block BYTE-IDENTICAL to base -- total escapes, not ties. ***
  63: #
  64: #    E1_aliasover  (1u << GDROM_W_FMT_B) -> (1u << GDROM_W_OVERREAD_B)          20/20
  65: #    E2_assignA    `d->warned |= GDROM_W_FMT;` -> `d->warned = GDROM_W_FMT;`    20/20
  66: #    E3_widenA     `& GDROM_W_FMT` -> `& (GDROM_W_FMT | GDROM_W_CMD)`           20/20
  67: #
  68: #  **E2 IS ONE CHARACTER AND IT IS THE WORST OF THE THREE.**  Site A still announces exactly
  69: #  once, so every Site-A row stays green -- but the ASSIGNMENT clears the other eight latches.
  70: #  A guest alternating a short TOC with a bad format then re-announces #489's diagnostic
  71: #  WITHOUT BOUND, and `fatal()` cannot be silenced by `-q`.  **No row observes any latch
  72: #  ACROSS a Site-A decline**, which is the structural gap: every latch row checks its OWN bit.
  73: #
  74: #  **E3 IS INVISIBLE ONLY BECAUSE OF ROW ORDER.**  Both two-site rows run Site A before
  75: #  Site B, so widening Site A's test is unobservable; *the same edit at Site B WOULD be
  76: #  caught* (`1,0`).  And pass 1's `S3 both` ran the OPPOSITE order -- the shipped rows
  77: #  inverted it and nothing recorded that.
  78: #
  79: #  SEVERITY: **coverage debt, not a floor breach**, per #464's precedent (`wdcperopcode`,
  80: #  `wdcsectorreset` -- the latter is E2's structural twin).  The floor's four clauses are all
  81: #  measured clean.  These are hypothetical future edits in uncovered cells, which is exactly
  82: #  what clause (e) exists to make FALSIFIABLE rather than argued.
  83: #
  84: #  A ONE-DECLARATION FIX FOR THE E1 CLASS EXISTS AND WAS CONTROLLED IN FIVE STATES -- a
  85: #  compile-time disjointness assert in this file's own `gdrom_w_fits_its_carriers` idiom:
  86: #  correct COMPILES; E1's alias REFUSED; `Ma_toclen`'s shape REFUSED; `M3shared`'s shape
  87: #  REFUSED; and E1 WITHOUT the assert COMPILES (the negative control).  **The addition-licence
  88: #  objection that declined the twelve alias mutants does NOT apply to a compile-time bound**,
  89: #  which costs no rows and fails CLOSED when a tenth bit arrives.  Filed for the owner.
  90: #
  91: #  NOT claimed:
  92: #    * collision with the other six latch bits -- see above, and now MEASURED as E1;
  93: #    * any latch's state ACROSS a decline at the other site (E2);
  94: #    * a latch test widened to cover a sibling bit at Site A (E3, order-dependent);
  95: #    * the tail's THIRD channel.  The added comments say the tail "zeroes d->cnt, clears
  96: #      COND_DATA_AVAIL and fires the completion event".  `cnt` has `fmt cnt`/`unk cnt` and
  97: #      the event has `fmt event`/`unk event`; **the COND_DATA_AVAIL clear has NO ROW.**  It
  98: #      is guest-observable through the `cond` read arm, and a row is buildable (stage
  99: #      DATA_AVAIL with a good TOC, decline, read cond).  It is NOT owed under the row-demand
 100: #      bound -- both `M_ret*` mutants are already killed twice over -- so this sentence is
 101: #      the explicit no-row discharge the CLAIM->ROW check requires, not an omission;
 102: #    * anything about the three exit(1) sites still live in dev_dreamcast_gdrom_access(),
 103: #      which are a different round; and nothing about exploitability of the heap overflow
 104: #      #489's guard prevents -- see artifacts/gdromexitsix/asan_m0_20260828/.
 105: #
 106: #  *** A ROW THAT FAILS **OPEN**, recorded because it is invisible in a green table. ***
 107: #  `latch cond` wants 0 and reads `REJ`, the unlatched `rejecting sector_count` diagnostic --
 108: #  which is OUTSIDE this diff, guest-drivable, and a natural future target of this very
 109: #  de-fatalisation campaign.  Reword that text and the regex matches nothing, the row goes
 110: #  permanently and silently GREEN, and no other row reads `REJ` with a non-zero want.  `HUH`
 111: #  is presence-pinned by `acceptA` and the 0x24 text by `fmt 0x24`; **`REJ` is pinned by
 112: #  nothing.**  A one-line presence row would close it.
 113: #  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 114: #
 115: #  *** EVERY ROW BELOW NAMES THE MUTANT IT KILLS AND THE OBSERVABLE THAT DIFFERS UNDER IT.
 116: #  Five of them were built and MEASURED by the pass-1 measuring seat before this file
 117: #  existed; three of those ESCAPED an earlier 13-row plan.  ***  The measured table is at
 118: #  artifacts/gdromexitsix/opus_r2full_20260828/ and every mutant name below matches it.
 119: #
 120: #  A NOTE ON WHY THE LATCH ROWS ARE NOT ONE ROW.  `M2nolatch` (bit tested, never set) and
 121: #  `M3shared` (both sites on one bit) are ORTHOGONAL -- measured, neither session catches
 122: #  the other's mutant.  A single "the latch fires once" row catches exactly one of them.
 123: #
 124: #  AND A NOTE ON THE STAGING OF `fmt cnt`, which would otherwise be VACUOUS.  `M_retA`'s
 125: #  `S3 both` session measured CNT=0x0 and ESCAPED, because the unknown command ran first and
 126: #  its tail had already zeroed `cnt`.  **The CNT channel discriminates only when d->cnt is
 127: #  non-zero on entry to the declined command.**  So `fmt cnt` writes CNTLO/CNTHI directly
 128: #  and then sends the bad packet -- one command, no prior dispatch.  The same construction
 129: #  is what `fmt event` needs, because `pending_irq` is STICKY and a staging TOC would
 130: #  pre-set the bit and kill that channel.
 131: 
 132: import os, pty, re, select, sys, time
 133: 
 134: if len(sys.argv) < 3:
 135:     sys.stderr.write("usage: gdrom_decline_probe.py <gxemul> <kernel>\n")
 136:     sys.exit(2)
 137: 
 138: BIN = sys.argv[1]
 139: KERNEL = sys.argv[2]
 140: 
 141: CODE = 0x8c001000
 142: SCRATCH = 0x8c001800
 143: 
 144: GDROM_DATA = 0xa05f7080
 145: GDROM_BUSY = 0xa05f7018
 146: GDROM_CNTLO = 0xa05f7090
 147: GDROM_CNTHI = 0xa05f7094
 148: GDROM_COND = 0xa05f709c
 149: 
 150: #  event 32 = bit 0 of the SECOND pending word.  Derived in #489 and re-checked here:
 151: #  SYSASIC_EVENT_GDROM is 32 in dreamcast_sysasicvar.h, and 32/32 = word 1.  The DMA rows in
 152: #  gdrom_default_probe.py read pending_irq[0] bit 14 -- a DIFFERENT event at a DIFFERENT
 153: #  address.  This is a one-address variant of that machinery, not a copy of it.
 154: SYSASIC_PEND1 = 0xa05f6904
 155: SYSASIC_GDROM_BIT = 0x00000001
 156: 
 157: IPRD_RESET = 0xda74
 158: LIVE = 0x12345678
 159: BRA_SELF = 0xaffe
 160: NOP = 0x0009
 161: 
 162: #  *** THE DECLINE REGEXES ARE ANCHORED ON `-- declined`, AND THAT IS MEASURED, NOT TIDY. ***
 163: #  The pass-1 measuring seat found that a bare `unimplemented data format` matches BOTH the
 164: #  upstream 0x24 warn-and-continue line AND the new Site-A decline, because the fix's message
 165: #  contains that exact substring.  A row on the bare form cannot tell them apart.
 166: FMT_DECL = re.compile(r"unimplemented data format 0x[0-9a-f]+ -- declined")
 167: CMD_DECL = re.compile(r"unimplemented command 0x[0-9a-f]+ -- declined")
 168: #  the upstream arm, which must stay byte-identical and must NOT match either regex above
 169: FMT_0X24 = re.compile(r"unimplemented data format 0x24\. Continuing anway")
 170: #  two unlatched diagnostics one guard further in, used as accept-side oracles
 171: REJ = re.compile(r"rejecting sector_count")
 172: HUH = re.compile(r"Huh\? GDROM data_len")
 173: 
 174: rows = []
 175: 
 176: 
 177: #  Row-name column: `%-14s` plus a literal space, and gate_sh_rounding.sh matches `^$v  `.
 178: #  A 13-character name leaves EXACTLY two spaces and matches; 14 leaves one and the check
 179: #  becomes unsatisfiable.  **The limit is 13.**  Measured -- gdrom_default_probe.py's own
 180: #  comment said "13 or 14" and was wrong about 13, corrected in #490.  Longest name here is
 181: #  10.  *** AN EARLIER DRAFT OF THIS LINE SAID 10 WHILE THE FILE HELD A 13-CHARACTER
 182: #  NAME -- caught by a length check on this very file before it ran.  Names here are now
 183: #  <= 10, and NO NAME IS A PREFIX OF ANOTHER FOLLOWED BY A SPACE: `acceptA` / `accept sil`
 184: #  rather than `acceptA` / `acceptA quiet`.  The two-space rule makes the latter safe, but
 185: #  relying on it is the padded-column trap wearing a seatbelt. ***
 186: def row(name, got, want, note=""):
 187:     ok = (got == want)
 188:     rows.append((name, ok))
 189:     print("%-14s %-12s %-12s %-40s %s" % (name, got, want, note,
 190:                                           "ok" if ok else "FAIL"))
 191:     return ok
 192: 
 193: 
 194: def session(cmds, settle=8.0, spin=2.0):
 195:     """Run one gxemul, feed debugger commands, return (transcript, alive, how).
 196: 
 197:     `how` is the DEATH MODE and it is not decoration: `alive` is False only when a read
 198:     raises OSError or returns empty INSIDE a budget window, so it is true of a HUNG
 199:     emulator, a FROZEN one, and -- measured -- one already killed after the last budget
 200:     check.  Every survival row here reads `how`, never `alive`."""
 201:     pid, fd = pty.fork()
 202:     if pid == 0:
 203:         os.execv(BIN, [BIN, "-V", "-x", "-E", "dreamcast", "-M", "64", KERNEL])
 204:         os._exit(127)
 205:     buf = ""
 206: 
 207:     def rd(t):
 208:         nonlocal buf
 209:         end = time.time() + t
 210:         while time.time() < end:
 211:             r, _, _ = select.select([fd], [], [], 0.25)
 212:             if not r:
 213:                 continue
 214:             try:
 215:                 d = os.read(fd, 65536)
 216:             except OSError:
 217:                 return False
 218:             if not d:
 219:                 return False
 220:             buf += d.decode("latin-1", "replace")
 221:         return True
 222: 
 223:     alive = rd(settle)
 224:     for i, c in enumerate(cmds):
 225:         try:
 226:             os.write(fd, (c + "\n").encode("latin-1"))
 227:         except OSError:
 228:             alive = False
 229:             break
 230:         if not rd(spin if i == len(cmds) - 1 else 0.35):
 231:             alive = False
 232:             break
 233:     try:
 234:         os.write(fd, b"quit\n")
 235:         rd(0.6)
 236:     except OSError:
 237:         pass
 238:     try:
 239:         os.close(fd)
 240:     except OSError:
 241:         pass
 242:     #  spelling COPIED VERBATIM from gdrom_toclen_probe.py -- two gdrom probes must not
 243:     #  disagree about what "died" means
 244:     how = "nostatus"
 245:     try:
 246:         _, stat = os.waitpid(pid, 0)
 247:         if os.WIFSIGNALED(stat):
 248:             how = "sig%d" % os.WTERMSIG(stat)
 249:         elif os.WIFEXITED(stat):
 250:             how = "exit%d" % os.WEXITSTATUS(stat)
 251:     except OSError:
 252:         pass
 253:     return buf, alive, how
 254: 
 255: 
 256: def printed(t, reg):
 257:     m = re.search(r"print\s+%s\s*\r?\n\s*(0x[0-9a-f]+)" % reg, t)
 258:     return m.group(1) if m else "absent"
 259: 
 260: 
 261: def MOVW_ST(n, m):
 262:     return 0x2001 | (n << 8) | (m << 4)
 263: 
 264: 
 265: def MOVW_LD(n, m):
 266:     return 0x6001 | (n << 8) | (m << 4)
 267: 
 268: 
 269: def MOVL_ST(n, m):
 270:     return 0x2002 | (n << 8) | (m << 4)
 271: 
 272: 
 273: class Addr(object):
 274:     """Hands out non-overlapping code addresses so planted runs cannot collide."""
 275: 
 276:     def __init__(self, base):
 277:         self.a = base
 278: 
 279:     def take(self, n_ops):
 280:         got = self.a
 281:         self.a += 2 * (n_ops + 2) + 0x20
 282:         return got
 283: 
 284: 
 285: def run(ops, at):
 286:     put = ["put h 0x%x, 0x%04x" % (at + 2 * i, op) for i, op in enumerate(ops)]
 287:     put += ["put h 0x%x, 0x%04x" % (at + 2 * len(ops), BRA_SELF),
 288:             "put h 0x%x, 0x%04x" % (at + 2 * len(ops) + 2, NOP)]
 289:     return put + ["pc=0x%x" % at, "step %d" % len(ops)]
 290: 
 291: 
 292: def store16(reg, val, A):
 293:     return ["r7=0x%x" % reg, "r8=0x%04x" % val] + run([MOVW_ST(7, 8)], A.take(1))
 294: 
 295: 
 296: #  *** THE OPERANDS WERE REVERSED HERE AND IT MADE THREE ROWS PASS VACUOUSLY. ***
 297: #  MOVW_LD(n, m) is `mov.w @Rm, Rn`.  This built MOVW_LD(1, dest) -- `mov.w @Rdest, R1`
 298: #  -- reading from the address in Rdest (0) into R1, then printing Rdest, which was
 299: #  never written.  **Every load16 row read 0x0 whatever the device held.**  `gdrom map`
 300: #  passed because "0x0" starts with "0x"; `fmt cnt` and `unk cnt` passed because they
 301: #  WANT 0x0.  Only `fmt stage`, whose wanted value is 0x98, could see it -- the whole
 302: #  argument for a row whose wanted value differs from its failure value.
 303: #
 304: #  AND IT CORRECTS A DIAGNOSIS ONE EDIT OLD: `devsig sh4` reading 0x0 was blamed on a
 305: #  16-bit access width the register would not serve.  FALSE -- the cause was this
 306: #  reversal.  That row was switched to load32 and worked, for a reason that was not the
 307: #  reason given, and a wrong reason gets reused.
 308: def load16(reg, A, dest=2):
 309:     return ["r1=0x%x" % reg, "r%d=0" % dest] + run([MOVW_LD(dest, 1)], A.take(1)) \
 310:         + ["print r%d" % dest]
 311: 
 312: 
 313: def load32(reg, A, dest=2):
 314:     return ["r1=0x%x" % reg, "r%d=0" % dest] + run([0x6002 | (1 << 4) | (dest << 8)],
 315:                                                    A.take(1)) + ["print r%d" % dest]
 316: 
 317: 
 318: def packet(words, A):
 319:     """Arm the device and push a 12-byte command packet, low halfword first."""
 320:     out = store16(GDROM_COND, 0xa0, A)
 321:     for w in words:
 322:         out += store16(GDROM_DATA, w, A)
 323:     return out
 324: 
 325: 
 326: #  A packet is SIX halfwords and dispatch fires on the sixth.  cmd[0] is the LOW byte of
 327: #  word 0, so 0x2030 means cmd[0]=0x30, cmd[1]=0x20 -- a pass-1 seat read this backwards and
 328: #  its conclusion happened to survive, which is why the byte order is stated here.
 329: def cmd(c0, c1=0x00, c10=0x0000):
 330:     return [c1 * 256 + c0, 0, 0, 0, 0, c10]
 331: 
 332: 
 333: GOOD_FMT = cmd(0x30, 0x20)       # the legal read-sectors packet
 334: BAD_FMT = cmd(0x30, 0x21)        # Site A: an unimplemented data format
 335: FMT_24 = cmd(0x30, 0x24)         # the upstream warn-and-continue arm -- NOT a decline
 336: BAD_CMD = cmd(0x31)              # Site B: an unimplemented command byte
 337: MOUNT = cmd(0x70)                # implemented, allocates nothing
 338: TOC_SHORT = [0x0014, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0]   # a TOC whose cnt will be wrong -> #489's decline
 339: 
 340: 
 341: # ---------------------------------------------------------------- identity controls
 342: #  A detector that cannot fail on a null medium is vacuous.  These three fail on a stale
 343: #  copy, a comment-only file, or a binary that never reached the device.
 344: 
 345: A = Addr(CODE)
 346: t, alive, how = session(["r7=0x%x" % SCRATCH, "r8=0x%08x" % LIVE]
 347:                         + run([MOVL_ST(7, 8)], A.take(1))
 348:                         + ["r1=0x%x" % SCRATCH, "r2=0"]
 349:                         + run([0x6002 | (1 << 4) | (2 << 8)], A.take(1)) + ["print r2"])
 350: row("live", printed(t, "r2") if alive else "died", "0x%x" % LIVE,
 351:     "LIVENESS: a guest store then a guest load, through RAM")
 352: 
 353: #  *** A 32-BIT LOAD, MASKED -- NOT A 16-BIT ONE.  MEASURED: the 16-bit form reads 0x0
 354: #  and this control FAILED on the probe's first run. ***  SH4_IPRD does not serve a
 355: #  halfword access here, so `mov.w` returns nothing and the row cannot tell a live
 356: #  device from a dead one -- a CONTROL that fails open is worse than no control.
 357: #  Copied from gdrom_toclen_probe.py, which had already solved it.
 358: #
 359: #  *** THIS IS THE THIRD TIME IN THIS FILE I INVENTED A VARIANT OF A SOLVED PROBLEM
 360: #  RATHER THAN COPYING THE WORKING ONE *** -- after a bogus write-1-to-clear pre-clear
 361: #  on the event rows, and a summed pair on `fmt 0x24`.  The sibling probe is the
 362: #  reference implementation; deviate from it only with a reason worth writing down.
 363: A = Addr(CODE)
 364: t, alive, how = session(load32(0xffd00010, A))
 365: g = printed(t, "r2")
 366: row("devsig sh4", ("0x%x" % (int(g, 16) & 0xffff)) if g.startswith("0x") else g,
 367:     "0x%x" % IPRD_RESET, "DEVSIG: the SH4 IPRD reset value; RAM would read 0")
 368: 
 369: A = Addr(CODE)
 370: t, alive, how = session(load16(GDROM_BUSY, A))
 371: g = printed(t, "r2")
 372: row("gdrom map", ("mapped" if g.startswith("0x") else g) if alive else "died", "mapped",
 373:     "the GDROM window is served, not a paddr fault")
 374: 
 375: #  *** CONTROL IS EMITTED HERE, OVER THE THREE IDENTITY ROWS ONLY -- NOT AT THE END OVER
 376: #  EVERYTHING.  MEASURED WHY ON THIS PROBE'S FIRST RUN: it printed CONTROL=DEAD because ONE
 377: #  PRODUCT ROW FAILED, which is exactly the conflation the gate section exists to prevent. ***
 378: #  The gate reads this to say "a run whose controls died is an APPARATUS failure and must not
 379: #  be read as product failures".  A CONTROL line that also goes DEAD on a product failure
 380: #  makes that sentence false and sends a reader hunting a broken rig for a real defect.
 381: #  `rows and` is load-bearing too: all([]) is True, so an empty list would certify a run that
 382: #  measured nothing.
 383: print("GDROM_DECL_CONTROL=%s"
 384:       % ("OK" if rows and all(ok for _, ok in rows) else "DEAD"))
 385: 
 386: 
 387: # ---------------------------------------------------------------- SITE A: the format decline
 388: #  kills: reinstating exit(1) at Site A          via: how
 389: #  kills: the arm not declining at all           via: the anchored decline text
 390: A = Addr(CODE)
 391: t, alive, how = session(packet(BAD_FMT, A))
 392: row("fmt surv", how, "exit0", "a bad data format must not end the host")
 393: row("fmt says", "%d" % len(FMT_DECL.findall(t)), "1",
 394:     "and it announces itself, exactly once")
 395: 
 396: #  kills: M_braceA -- `break` moved INSIDE the latch `if`, so the SECOND identical bad
 397: #         format falls through into sector_nr.  MEASURED escaping a 13-row plan, because
 398: #         "announces once" reads 1 either way.
 399: #  via:   a DECLINE channel read after the SECOND command, not the announce count
 400: A = Addr(CODE)
 401: t, alive, how = session(packet(BAD_FMT, A) + packet(BAD_FMT, A))
 402: row("fmt x2", "%d" % len(FMT_DECL.findall(t)), "1",
 403:     "two bad formats, ONE line -- the latch holds")
 404: row("latch cond", "%d" % len(REJ.findall(t)), "0",
 405:     "and the SECOND one still declines -- no fallthrough to sector_nr")
 406: 
 407: #  kills: M_retA -- `break` -> `return`, which skips the common tail.
 408: #  via:   d->cnt, which the tail zeroes.  *** STAGED WITH NO PRIOR DISPATCH: the CNT channel
 409: #         discriminates ONLY when d->cnt is non-zero on entry, and M_retA measured CNT=0x0
 410: #         (escaping) in a session where an earlier command had already zeroed it. ***
 411: #         pending_irq is STICKY, so the same single-command staging is what `fmt event`
 412: #         needs -- a staging TOC would pre-set the bit and make that row vacuous.
 413: A = Addr(CODE)
 414: #  *** `fmt cnt` WANTS 0x0 -- AND A FAILED READ ALSO GIVES 0x0.  So the staging is read
 415: #  BACK IN THE SAME SESSION FIRST: without `fmt stage` proving 0x98 actually landed,
 416: #  `fmt cnt` is green whether the tail zeroed the count or the read simply did not
 417: #  work.  The pair is the oracle; neither half is one alone. ***
 418: t, alive, how = session(store16(GDROM_CNTLO, 0x98, A) + store16(GDROM_CNTHI, 0x01, A)
 419:                         + load16(GDROM_CNTLO, A, dest=3)
 420:                         + packet(BAD_FMT, A) + load16(GDROM_CNTLO, A))
 421: row("fmt stage", printed(t, "r3"), "0x98",
 422:     "the staged count is really there BEFORE the command")
 423: row("fmt cnt", printed(t, "r2"), "0x0",
 424:     "and the tail zeroed it -- a return would have left 0x98")
 425: 
 426: A = Addr(CODE)
 427: t, alive, how = session(packet(BAD_FMT, A) + load32(SYSASIC_PEND1, A))
 428: g = printed(t, "r2")
 429: row("fmt event", ("set" if (int(g, 16) & SYSASIC_GDROM_BIT) else "clear")
 430:     if g.startswith("0x") else g, "set",
 431:     "a declined command still reports completion -- a return would hang the guest")
 432: 
 433: #  *** NO PRE-CLEAR OF THE PENDING REGISTER, AND THE OBVIOUS 'CAREFUL' FORM IS WRONG. ***
 434: #  An earlier draft of these two rows wrote 0 to SYSASIC_PEND1 first, to clear the bit
 435: #  before the command set it.  `dev_dreamcast_asic.c`'s write arm is
 436: #  `d->pending_irq[r] &= ~idata` -- **WRITE-1-TO-CLEAR** -- so writing 0 clears NOTHING.
 437: #  The bit would never have been cleared, and the row would pass whether or not the
 438: #  decline fired the event: VACUOUS, and vacuous in the direction that looks diligent.
 439: #  It was also a 16-bit store to a 32-bit register.  The correct construction needs no
 440: #  clear at all: each session is a FRESH process, so the bit starts clear and only the
 441: #  dispatched command can set it.  Copied from gdrom_toclen_probe.py's `bad event`,
 442: #  which had already solved this.  Found by desk-check against the device source
 443: #  before this file was ever run.
 444: #  kills: an accept-set growth that folds the upstream 0x24 arm into the decline.
 445: #  via:   the 0x24 line is byte-identical upstream text and must match NEITHER new regex.
 446: A = Addr(CODE)
 447: t, alive, how = session(packet(FMT_24, A))
