   1: #!/usr/bin/env python3
   2: #
   3: #  #497 widthkillclass -- the detector.
   4: #
   5: #  CONTRACT (vacuity-floor clause (e)) -- which edits this detector is designed
   6: #  to catch, and which it is NOT:
   7: #
   8: #    CATCHES
   9: #      * removal or reinstatement of ANY of the six width kills, at any one of
  10: #        the six sites individually (the `sw surv' rows are per site);
  11: #      * narrowing the decline so it applies to writes only, i.e.
  12: #        `len != 8' -> `len != 8 && writeflag == MEM_WRITE'  (the `lw surv' rows);
  13: #      * ANY decline that does not reach the function's common tail -- an early
  14: #        `return', or `return 0' (which memory_rw coerces to -1 and turns into a
  15: #        guest EXCEPTION_DBE).  The `lw surv' rows check the VALUE the declined
  16: #        read returns, not merely that the host survived: the destination
  17: #        register is preset to a sentinel, so "tail ran, wrote 0" and "load
  18: #        never completed" are distinguishable.  A survival-only oracle at a
  19: #        refusal-effect site is vacuous by construction, and this row was one
  20: #        until the d_returnzero mutant was built;
  21: #      * deleting the latch, or dropping the `d->warned |= BIT' set, so the
  22: #        message floods  (the `sw lat1' rows COUNT; they do not test presence);
  23: #      * making two or more sites share a latch bit  (`xsite 6msg');
  24: #      * mis-attributing a window's message to another window (`xsite order');
  25: #      * growth of the width predicate that swallows LEGAL 8-byte accesses
  26: #        (the `accept' rows, probed at an offset with bit 3 SET so that a
  27: #        `|| (relative_addr & 8)' tightening reddens them);
  28: #      * silent removal of the six READ kills or of the final catch-all, which
  29: #        this round deliberately does NOT touch  (`<s> ld kill' x6, `other kill').
  30: #
  31: #    *** THE TWO PARAGRAPHS ABOVE ARE #497's AND #499's AND ARE NO LONGER TRUE OF THIS
  32: #        FILE.  #513 REMOVED ALL SIX READ KILLS -- five arms became ACCEPTs and
  33: #        CRIME_RE_TEX became a latched decline -- so `<s> ld kill' x6 NO LONGER EXIST
  34: #        as row names, and an 8-byte `ld' to those windows no longer ends the host.
  35: #        The contract below is the LIVE one; the text above is kept, with this
  36: #        correction beside it, because a reader arriving from #497's or #499's records
  37: #        needs to see which half moved and when.  Found by the pass-2 panel, which read
  38: #        the contract against the diff and reported it stale. ***
  39: #
  40: #    *** AND IT PINS THE ROUND'S CENTRAL DISCLOSURE, RATHER THAN ASSERTING IT.
  41: #        The width guard is evaluated FIRST, so after #497 a 4-byte read
  42: #        DECLINES -- but an ordinary 8-byte `ld' to each of the six windows
  43: #        STILL ENDS THE HOST, because len == 8 passes the width guard and falls
  44: #        into the read arm's exit(1) (dev_sgi_re.c lines 245 262 279 296 346
  45: #        362).  Fixing six width kills leaves EXACTLY six read kills at the same
  46: #        six offsets.  The `<s> ld kill' rows measure that at every window, so a
  47: #        future reader cannot conclude from `lw surv' that reads were made safe.
  48: #        Filed as sgirekills.  *** ITS WITNESS IS THIS FILE: the six `<site> ld kill`
  49: #        rows below ARE rung-3 symptom demonstrations of that defect, and the ledger's
  50: #        witness field names them.  This comment used to say the row `has no witness
  51: #        yet' -- written before those rows existed and left standing after they shipped,
  52: #        so the file denied being the witness it had become.  Found by the codex seat. ***
  53: #
  54: #    DOES NOT CATCH
  55: #      * anything in DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_de) or (sgi_mte), including sgi_de's own
  56: #        width kill (accept set {4,8}, filed sgidewidth) and sgi_mte's
  57: #        unreachable one (filed sgimtewidth);
  58: #      * the LINEAR out-of-bounds index itself.  Observing re_linear_b[0] being
  59: #        clobbered needs a READ path, and every read arm here still exit(1)s.
  60: #        *** THAT CLAUSE IS #497's AND IS FALSE SINCE #513: five read arms ACCEPT and
  61: #        CRIME_RE_TEX declines, so a READ PATH NOW EXISTS.  Whether that makes the
  62: #        aliasing rowable is a QUESTION FOR sgilinearoob, not a claim made here.
  63: #        The aliasing is pinned by offsetof in PRETABLE.out section 1.2, not by
  64: #        a row.  Owed to sgirekills.
  65: #
  66: #    ONE THING THE CONTRACT UNDERSTATED, corrected before shipping rather than
  67: #    left as a modest-sounding false claim: an earlier draft said there is NO
  68: #    anchored cell-position row.  There is.  Each `accept' row matches the arm's
  69: #    own debug() text INCLUDING THE INDEX AND THE VALUE -- e.g.
