 135:       each found a breach of this one class and the closing pass must verify an
 136:       ENUMERATION rather than hunt cells.  A bypass `|| <cond>' on the latch is
 137:       observable iff some session reaches `default:' at least twice with <cond>
 138:       TRUE on a later reach.  The two-draw sessions and what they pin:
 139:           op bit-tests      `|| (op & MASK)', EVERY nonzero 32-bit MASK -- CLOSED.
 140:                             The nine second-reach op literals union to 0xffffffff
 141:                             and `op & MASK != 0' is monotone in MASK.
 142:           op inequality     `|| (op != V)' -- CLOSED (>= 2 distinct second-reach ops).
 143:           the ELEVEN registers draw_primitive() reads on a decline path --
 144:                             drawmode, src_mode, dst_mode, rop, stipple_mode, and the
 145:                             six XFER-block registers addr_src, strd_src, step_x,
 146:                             step_y, addr_dst, strd_dst: every nonzero bit-test --
 147:                             CLOSED by `dec regs' (all-ones on a POINT double-draw).
 148:                             (Pass 15: the six were added after `|| step_x' MEASURED
 149:                             50/50 -- the pass-14 all-ones drawmode had enabled the
 150:                             block that reads them.)
 151:           op == 0x00000000  the reset value -- CLOSED by `dec regs'.
 152:           latch-field bits  0-8 by `xlatch all'; 9 is the latch itself.  (DERIVED,
 153:                             not mutant-measured: no `|| (d->warned & bit)' cell was
 154:                             ever built.  Pass 16 named it; filed.  An unmeasured
 155:                             closure must not render like a measured one.)
 156:           operand zero/nonzero on x1..pattern -- CLOSED by `dec ops'.
 157:       DELIBERATELY NOT COVERED, and unclosable by rows -- an escape here is OUTSIDE
 158:       THE CONTRACT, not a breach.  *** PASS 15 GENERALISED THIS LIST after three seats
 159:       showed the pass-14 form was a per-class claim dressed as a universal: it named
 160:       equality/range/conjunction/sub-bit/compound and a seat produced a same-field
 161:       bit RELATION on op (`(op & 1) != ((op >> 1) & 1)', false on every literal) that
 162:       it did not name.  The honest general statement: ***
 163:           THE ONLY CLOSED PREDICATE SHAPES ARE (a) monotone nonzero bit-tests
 164:           `x & MASK' on op and the eleven registers, (b) op inequality, (c) the one
 165:           equality `op == 0', and (d) zero/nonzero on the seven operands.  EVERY OTHER
 166:           predicate on op, a register or an operand is uncovered by construction,
 167:           because the sessions pin at most TWO values per REGISTER or OPERAND field
 168:           (op is the exception: ten distinct second-reach literals, so equality to
 169:           any of those ten is incidentally closed -- pass 16 corrected 'op == 0' to
 170:           that, the safe direction): equality to any
 171:           unpinned value, masked equality, ranges, bit relations, arbitrary Boolean
 172:           functions of bits, and compounds across fields.  Each is an infinite family
 173:           and each new two-draw session pins one member.  Measured limits: `op ==
 174:           0x07000000' and `fg & 0x80' (table 12, both still full-score).
 175:       AND ONE WORDING FROM A SEAT: the observability criterion assumes the latch is
 176:       SET on the later reach, i.e. the FIRST reach of that session declined.  Every
 177:       literal used here reaches `default:'; a future session whose first draw matched
 178:       an implemented arm would silently not observe.  Stated so nobody re-derives it.
 179:       Each of those families is infinite or combinatorial, and each new two-draw
 180:       session pins one member.  Two of them were MEASURED as 47/47 in pass 13
 181:       (`op == 0x07000000', `fg & 0x80'); they are recorded here as the limit, not
 182:       hunted. ***
 183:     * anything about WIDTH -- that is sgi_de_width_probe.py, which also owns the
 184:       `tail_acc*' rows that pin draw_primitive's REACHABILITY;
 185:     * the drawing itself.  A declined op draws nothing, and nothing here asserts
 186:       what a correct POINT or TRIANGLE would have rendered.
 187: """
 188: 
 189: import os
 190: import pty
 191: import re
 192: import select
 193: import sys
 194: import time
 195: 
 196: BIN = sys.argv[1] if len(sys.argv) > 1 else "build/gxemul"
 197: LABEL = sys.argv[2] if len(sys.argv) > 2 else "local"
 198: 
 199: #  A load file is REQUIRED by -V ... 0x80001000:<file>.  The in-tree fixture if
 200: #  it exists, /dev/null if not -- the same fallback the sibling probes use.
 201: DUMMY = "dummy_kernel.bin" if os.path.exists("dummy_kernel.bin") else "/dev/null"
 202: 
 203: CODE = 0xffffffff80001000
 204: DE = 0xffffffffb5002000              # sgi_de        @ phys 0x15002000
 205: #  *** DE_STATUS IS GONE.  It was UNUSED after devsig attempt 3, and it was
 206: #  MISLABELLED throughout: phys 0x15003000 is the sgi_mte window; sgi_de_status
 207: #  is at 0x15004000 (dev_sgi_re.c:36-37).  A seat found both in one sentence. ***
 208: RE = 0xffffffffb5001000              # sgi_re        @ phys 0x15001000
 209: UNMAP_W = 0x800                      # #499's unmapped-WRITE window -> UNMAP_WR
 210: UNMAP_R = 0x900                      # #499's unmapped-READ  window -> UNMAP_RD
 211: 
 212: #  0x060 is CRIME_DE_PRIMITIVE; 0x800 is the START alias.  A single access to
 213: #  0x860 therefore SETS the op and TRIGGERS the draw -- which is why the round's
 214: #  first witness was confounded: a START-aliased WRITE also writes the register,
 215: #  so the trigger value BECOMES op.  That is a feature here, not a hazard: it is
 216: #  exactly how a guest reaches draw_primitive in one instruction.
 217: PRIM_START = 0x860
 218: PRIM_ONLY = 0x060
 219: DRAWMODE = 0x018                     # CRIME_DE_DRAWMODE 0x2018, device-relative
 220: #  The OTHER four registers draw_primitive() reads before the switch, device-relative.
 221: #  #500 pass 14: every one of the five is ZERO on every path that reaches `default:'
 222: #  in every session below this line's introduction -- see `dec regs'.
 223: MODE_SRC = 0x000                     # CRIME_DE_MODE_SRC     0x2000
 224: MODE_DST = 0x008                     # CRIME_DE_MODE_DST     0x2008
 225: STIPPLE_MODE = 0x0c0                 # CRIME_DE_STIPPLE_MODE 0x20c0
 226: DE_ROP = 0x1b0                       # CRIME_DE_ROP          0x21b0
 227: #  #500 pass 15: the SIX registers the XFER block reads once drawmode has XFER_EN set --
 228: #  which `dec regs' all-ones drawmode SETS.  The pass-14 closure opened the path to six
 229: #  more zero registers; `|| step_x' was predicted by two readers and MEASURED 50/50.
 230: XFER_ADDR_SRC = 0x0a0                # CRIME_DE_XFER_ADDR_SRC 0x20a0
 231: XFER_STRD_SRC = 0x0a4                # CRIME_DE_XFER_STRD_SRC 0x20a4
 232: XFER_STEP_X = 0x0a8                  # CRIME_DE_XFER_STEP_X   0x20a8
 233: XFER_STEP_Y = 0x0ac                  # CRIME_DE_XFER_STEP_Y   0x20ac
 234: XFER_ADDR_DST = 0x0b0                # CRIME_DE_XFER_ADDR_DST 0x20b0
 235: XFER_STRD_DST = 0x0b4                # CRIME_DE_XFER_STRD_DST 0x20b4
 236: XFER_EN = "0x00200000"               # DE_DRAWMODE_XFER_EN
 237: XFER_LINE = "sgi_de: XFER_EN for LINE op?"
 238: 
 239: #  The seven operands the latch's guarded body reads, and where a guest sets
 240: #  them.  Device-relative (the DE handler adds 0x2000 back).
 241: VERTEX_0 = 0x070                     # CRIME_DE_X_VERTEX_0: x1 = v>>16, y1 = v & 0x7ff
 242: VERTEX_1 = 0x074                     # CRIME_DE_X_VERTEX_1: x2, y2
 243: DE_FG = 0x0d0                        # CRIME_DE_FG
 244: DE_BG = 0x0d8                        # CRIME_DE_BG
 245: STIPPLE_PAT = 0x0c4                  # CRIME_DE_STIPPLE_PAT
 246: START_ONLY = 0x800
 247: 
 248: OP_NOP = 0x00000000
 249: OP_B_SELF = 0x1000ffff
 250: OP_SB_T0_A3 = 0xa0e80000             # sb   $t0, 0($a3)
 251: OP_SW_T0_A3 = 0xace80000             # sw   $t0, 0($a3)
 252: OP_LW_V0_AT = 0x8c220000             # lw   $v0, 0($at)
 253: OP_LBU_V0_AT = 0x90220000            # lbu  $v0, 0($at)
 254: 
 255: #  DRAW matches the kill AND the decline -- the prefix is deliberately kept, so
 256: #  that #499's `hi_draw'/`hi_rd_draw' rows are not silently blinded.  That makes
 257: #  it useful for "did a draw happen at all" and USELESS for "was it declined".
 258: DRAW = "sgi_de: UNIMPLEMENTED drawing op"
 259: 
 260: 
 261: def declined(opval):
 262:     """The FULL declined literal for one op value.
 263: 
 264:     *** THE `decl' ROWS ORIGINALLY COUNTED `DRAW', AND A REVIEW SEAT FOUND THAT
 265:     VACUOUS: it is a substring the decline SHARES WITH THE PRE-FIX KILL, so a
 266:     mutant reverting the message text passes, and the row cannot tell a fixed
 267:     build from an unfixed one.  A `DECLINED' constant was defined in this file
 268:     and never used -- the constant existed and the rows did not reach for it. ***
 269: 
 270:     This form pins the op VALUE and the `-- declined.  (once)' suffix together,
 271:     so it is un-fakeable by either a text revert or a wrong-op decline.
 272:     """
 273:     return ("UNIMPLEMENTED drawing op = %s, x1=0 y1=0 x2=0 y2=0"
 274:             " fg=0x0 bg=0x0 pattern=0x00000000 -- declined.  (once) ]" % opval)
 275: SENTINEL = 0xdeadbeef
 276: 
 277: rows = []
 278: 
 279: 
 280: def row(name, got, want, note=""):
 281:     #  <= 15 chars: the name column is %-16s and the gate's per-row pattern needs
 282:     #  TWO spaces after the name.  A 16-char name emits ONE and the gate check
 283:     #  becomes unsatisfiable -- a RED gate on a GREEN probe.  A sibling round
 284:     #  shipped exactly that and it was found by a review seat, not by the gate.
 285:     #  *** A DUPLICATE ROW NAME IS A CORRUPT INSTRUMENT, AND IT ONCE REPORTED
 286:     #  38/38 GREEN.  A bad splice duplicated seven rows; every one of them passed
 287:     #  twice and the run looked like the best table in the file's history.  The
 288:     #  gate would have caught it -- its per-row check wants exactly 1 -- but only
 289:     #  after a 15-minute run and only as an unexplained got=2.  One line here
 290:     #  fails at the point of construction instead. ***
 291:     assert name not in [n for n, _ in rows], (
 292:         "DUPLICATE ROW NAME %r -- the probe is corrupt, not merely wrong" % name)
 293:     assert len(name) <= 15, (
 294:         "row name %r is %d chars; the gate pattern needs <= 15" % (name, len(name)))
 295:     ok = (got == want)
 296:     rows.append((name, ok))
 297:     print("%-16s %-14s %-14s %-44s %s" % (name, str(got), str(want), note,
 298:                                           "ok" if ok else "FAIL"))
 299:     return ok
 300: 
 301: 
 302: def session(cmds, settle=1.5, spin=1.4):
 303:     pid, fd = pty.fork()
 304:     if pid == 0:
 305:         os.execv(BIN, [BIN, "-V", "-x", "-E", "sgi", "-e", "ip32", "-M", "64",
 306:                        "0x80001000:" + DUMMY])
 307:         os._exit(127)
 308:     buf = ""
 309: 
 310:     def rd(t):
 311:         nonlocal buf
 312:         end = time.time() + t
 313:         while time.time() < end:
 314:             r, _, _ = select.select([fd], [], [], 0.1)
 315:             if not r:
 316:                 continue
 317:             try:
 318:                 d = os.read(fd, 65536)
 319:                 if not d:
 320:                     return False
 321:                 buf += d.decode("latin-1", "replace")
 322:             except OSError:
 323:                 return False
 324:         return True
 325: 
 326:     alive = rd(settle)
 327:     for i, c in enumerate(cmds):
 328:         try:
 329:             os.write(fd, (c + "\n").encode("latin-1"))
 330:         except OSError:
 331:             alive = False
 332:             break
 333:         if not rd(spin if i == len(cmds) - 1 else 0.25):
 334:             alive = False
 335:             break
 336:     try:
 337:         os.write(fd, b"quit\n")
 338:         rd(0.4)
 339:     except OSError:
 340:         pass
 341:     try:
 342:         os.close(fd)
 343:     except OSError:
 344:         pass
 345:     how = "nostatus"
 346:     try:
 347:         _, stat = os.waitpid(pid, 0)
 348:         if os.WIFSIGNALED(stat):
 349:             how = "sig%d" % os.WTERMSIG(stat)
 350:         elif os.WIFEXITED(stat):
 351:             how = "exit%d" % os.WEXITSTATUS(stat)
 352:     except OSError:
 353:         pass
 354:     return buf.replace("\r", ""), alive, how
 355: 
 356: 
 357: def printed(t, reg):
 358:     m = re.search(r"print\s+%s\s*\n\s*(0x[0-9a-f]+)" % reg, t)
 359:     if not m:
 360:         return "absent"
 361:     return "0x%x" % int(m.group(1), 16)
 362: 
 363: 
 364: class Addr(object):
 365:     """A FRESH code address per instruction sequence.
 366: 
 367:     `step' advances pc, so a second sequence written to the SAME address finds pc
 368:     already set there, the assignment is skipped, and the guest executes the
 369:     following nop instead of the store.  A sibling probe lost a row to exactly
 370:     that, so the hazard is designed out rather than remembered.
 371:     """
 372: 
 373:     def __init__(self, base):
 374:         self.a = base
 375: 
 376:     def take(self, n):
 377:         got = self.a
 378:         self.a += 4 * (n + 2) + 0x20
 379:         return got
 380: 
 381: 
 382: def run_ops(ops, at):
 383:     put = ["put w 0x%x, 0x%08x" % (at + 4 * i, op) for i, op in enumerate(ops)]
 384:     put += ["put w 0x%x, 0x%08x" % (at + 4 * len(ops), OP_B_SELF),
 385:             "put w 0x%x, 0x%08x" % (at + 4 * len(ops) + 4, OP_NOP)]
 386:     return put + ["pc=0x%x" % at, "step %d" % len(ops)]
 387: 
 388: 
 389: def store(A, addr, val, op=OP_SW_T0_A3):
 390:     return ["a3=0x%x" % addr, "t0=%s" % val] + run_ops([op], A.take(1))
 391: 
 392: 
 393: def load(A, addr, op=OP_LW_V0_AT):
 394:     #  Preset to a SENTINEL, never 0: preset to 0 and a row cannot tell "the
 395:     #  access completed and returned 0" from "the access never happened".
 396:     return ["at=0x%x" % addr, "v0=0x%x" % SENTINEL] + run_ops([op], A.take(1))
 397: 
 398: 
 399: print("#500 sgideprimop DETECTOR -- binary=%s label=%s" % (BIN, LABEL))
 400: print("%-16s %-14s %-14s %-44s %s" % ("row", "got", "want", "note", ""))
 401: 
 402: # ------------------------------------------------------------ controls (c)
 403: #  Clause (ii) of the witness ladder: an access witness owes a LIVENESS control
 404: #  (a known value returns through the same decode) AND a DEVICE-SIGNATURE
 405: #  control (the value could only have come from the device -- RAM would answer
 406: #  differently).  One is not enough: a sibling probe's first draft returned 0x0
 407: #  everywhere WITH its RAM control green.
 408: A = Addr(CODE)
 409: t, alive, how = session(store(A, 0xffffffff80002000, val="0x5a5a5a5a")
 410:                         + load(A, 0xffffffff80002000) + ["print v0"])
 411: row("live", printed(t, "v0") if alive else "died", "0x5a5a5a5a",
 412:     "RAM round-trips through the same decode -- the probe can see anything at all")
 413: 
 414: #  *** THIS CONTROL TOOK THREE ATTEMPTS AND THE FIRST TWO ARE WORTH RECORDING,
 415: #  because both failed in ways the carrier already warns about. ***
 416: #
 417: #  ATTEMPT 1 asserted only `printed(...) != "absent"' -- that SOMETHING answered --
 418: #  while its note claimed "a DEVICE answers, not RAM".  RAM answers too, so the row
 419: #  could not distinguish them and the note asserted a test that was not being made:
 420: #  a vacuous row carrying a false record.  A review seat caught it.
 421: #
 422: #  ATTEMPT 2 tried a write-that-does-not-stick at DE_STATUS+0, on the assumption
 423: #  that a device register would not return what was written to it.  MEASURED: it
 424: #  returns 0x5a, exactly what was written.  That address round-trips like RAM, so
 425: #  the premise was false and the row correctly went RED on a good build.
 426: #
 427: #  ATTEMPT 3, and this one is measured rather than assumed: use a channel RAM
 428: #  CANNOT PRODUCE AT ALL -- a device DIAGNOSTIC.  A byte store to sgi_de trips
 429: #  #498's width guard and emits "[ sgi_de: unimplemented len=1 -- declined.
 430: #  (once) ]".  No memory location emits text.  It is also INDEPENDENT of
 431: #  draw_primitive, so it controls for "a device is there" without borrowing the
 432: #  channel the rows under test use.
 433: #
 434: #  *** AND THE FORK ALREADY HAD ONE.  I INVENTED THREE TIMES WITHOUT LOOKING. ***
 435: #  sgi_re_width_probe.py carries `devsig stat': CRIME_DE_STATUS reads the fixed
 436: #  architectural value 0x1e000000, and RAM would read 0.  That is a cleaner
 437: #  signature than this one -- a constant the hardware defines, needing no
 438: #  diagnostic text -- and it was sitting in a sibling file the whole time.
 439: #  Attempt 2 failed at DE_STATUS+0 for exactly the reason that row would have
 440: #  taught me: the discriminating offset is not the base.
 441: #
 442: #  This row is KEPT rather than replaced, because two independent channels are
 443: #  worth more than one: a fixed-value read and a device-only diagnostic fail for
 444: #  different reasons.  But the lesson is the project's own standing rule --
 445: #  GREP THE FORK FOR THE EXISTING IDIOM BEFORE INVENTING -- and it cost three
 446: #  attempts here on the same day it was applied successfully twice elsewhere.
 447: A = Addr(CODE)
 448: t, alive, how = session(store(A, DE + 0x0, val="0x5a", op=OP_SB_T0_A3))
 449: row("devsig", t.count("sgi_de: unimplemented len=1 -- declined.  (once) ]"), 1,
 450:     "a byte access makes the DEVICE speak -- RAM emits no text, ever")
 451: 
 452: # ------------------------------------------------------- the two named killers
 453: #  POINT and TRIANGLE are DOCUMENTED primitives that the model does not
 454: #  implement.  Each gets survival AND a positive post-draw observable, because a
 455: #  survival-only oracle cannot tell a decline from an exit(0).
 456: for nm, opval in (("pt", "0x00000000"), ("tri", "0x02000000")):
 457:     A = Addr(CODE)
 458:     t, alive, how = session(store(A, DE + PRIM_START, val=opval)
 459:                             + load(A, 0xffffffff80002000) + ["print v0"])
 460:     row(nm + " surv", how, "exit0",
 461:         "%s (%s) is declined, not fatal" % (nm.upper(), opval))
 462:     row(nm + " live", printed(t, "v0") if alive else "died", "0x0",
 463:         "...and the guest RUNS ON -- kills a death however it is spelled")
 464:     row(nm + " decl", t.count(declined(opval)), 1,
 465:         "...announced ONCE with the FULL literal -- kills a text revert")
 466: 
 467: # ------------------------------------------------- the reachability headline
 468: #  op reads the RESET value, so a START access with CRIME_DE_PRIMITIVE never
 469: #  written draws POINT.  This is the row that pins "it need only omit a good
 470: #  value", and nothing else in the tree asserts it.
 471: A = Addr(CODE)
 472: t, alive, how = session(store(A, DE + START_ONLY, val="0x00000000")
 473:                         + load(A, 0xffffffff80002000) + ["print v0"])
 474: row("reset surv", how, "exit0",
 475:     "START with PRIMITIVE never written -- the reset value IS POINT")
 476: row("reset live", printed(t, "v0") if alive else "died", "0x0",
 477:     "...and the guest RUNS ON")
 478: row("reset decl", t.count(declined("0x00000000")), 1,
 479:     "...announced with the FULL literal -- the draw really happened")
 480: 
 481: # ------------------------------------------------------------- the LATCH rows
 482: #  TWO distinct defaulted primitives in ONE session, wanting EXACTLY one message.
 483: #  *** THIS IS THE ROW THAT CATCHES THE FLOOD MUTANT (dropping the `|='), which a
 484: #  "at least one message" row passes.  The SILENT mutant (dropping the `!') is
 485: #  caught by the `* decl' rows above.  Two different latch mutants, two different
 486: #  killers, and only this one is exact-count. ***
 487: A = Addr(CODE)
 488: t, alive, how = session(store(A, DE + PRIM_START, val="0x00000000")
 489:                         + store(A, DE + PRIM_START, val="0x02000000")
 490:                         + load(A, 0xffffffff80002000) + ["print v0"])
 491: row("two once", t.count(DRAW), 1,
 492:     "TWO distinct declined ops, ONE message -- kills a dropped |= (flood)")
 493: row("two live", printed(t, "v0") if alive else "died", "0x0",
 494:     "...and the guest survives both")
 495: 
 496: # ------------------------------------------------------------ the READ arm
 497: #  `if (startFlag)' is not conditioned on MEM_WRITE, so a START-aliased READ
 498: #  draws too -- with whatever op the register already holds, i.e. the reset
 499: #  value.  A repair applied to the write direction only escapes every row above.
 500: A = Addr(CODE)
 501: t, alive, how = session(load(A, DE + START_ONLY) + ["print v0"])
 502: row("rd surv", how, "exit0",
 503:     "a START-aliased READ also draws, and is declined")
 504: row("rd decl", t.count(declined("0x00000000")), 1,
 505:     "...announced with the FULL literal -- kills a write-only repair")
 506: 
 507: # --------------------------------------------------- the CROSS-LATCH row
 508: #  *** EVERY ROW ABOVE RUNS IN A FRESH SESSION WITH NO PRIOR DEVICE TRAFFIC, AND A
 509: #  REVIEW SEAT POINTED OUT WHAT THAT CANNOT SEE: a bit-value mutant onto an
 510: #  ALREADY-USED bit (1 << 6 is #498's SGIRE_W_DE_LEN, 1 << 8 is #499's UNMAP_RD)
 511: #  leaves those latches CLEAR in a fresh session, so the announce still fires and
 512: #  every row above passes.  The collision is only visible once something else has
 513: #  ALREADY SET the colliding bit. ***
 514: #
 515: #  It matters because the four sgi_re/sgi_de/sgi_mte/sgi_de_status handlers share
 516: #  ONE malloc'd struct, so `d->warned' is shared ACROSS DEVICES -- #498 measured a
 517: #  reused bit at 23/25, and BOTH #499 and this round were originally designed for
 518: #  1 << 7.  The collision was caught by a human listing the enum, which is exactly
 519: #  the kind of catch that should not be relied upon.
 520: #
 521: #  So: fire the #498 width latch FIRST with a byte access, THEN trigger the draw,
 522: #  and require the prim-op decline ANYWAY.  Under a colliding bit the first access
 523: #  pre-sets it and this row goes silent.
 524: A = Addr(CODE)
 525: t, alive, how = session(store(A, DE + 0x0, val="0x5a", op=OP_SB_T0_A3)
 526:                         + store(A, DE + PRIM_START, val="0x02000000")
 527:                         + load(A, 0xffffffff80002000) + ["print v0"])
 528: row("xlatch decl", t.count(declined("0x02000000")), 1,
 529:     "prim-op decline fires even AFTER another latch set -- kills a shared bit")
 530: row("xlatch live", printed(t, "v0") if alive else "died", "0x0",
 531:     "...and the guest survives the composed session")
 532: 
 533: #  *** AND ONE PRE-SET LATCH IS ONE CELL, NOT THE CLASS -- SAME MISTAKE, SAME
 534: #  ROUND, ONE ROW OVER.  Two seats found it independently. ***
 535: #
 536: #  `xlatch decl' above pre-fires exactly ONE colliding bit, #498's 1 << 6.  The
 537: #  enum's OTHER already-used bits are #499's 1 << 7 (UNMAP_WR) and 1 << 8
 538: #  (UNMAP_RD), and NO row in this file raises either before drawing -- so
 539: #  aliasing SGIRE_W_PRIM_OP onto 1 << 7 or 1 << 8 left all 30 rows green while
 540: #  making the decline go silent after ordinary unmapped traffic.  A one-token
 541: #  edit to the very constant this round ADDED: floor clause (d), not coverage
 542: #  debt.
 543: #
 544: #  The four handlers share one `d->warned', so the unmapped windows on sgi_re
 545: #  set latches that the sgi_de draw path then tests.  Raising BOTH in one
 546: #  session closes the remaining pair; with 1 << 6 above, every already-used bit
 547: #  in the enum is now pre-set by some row.
 548: A = Addr(CODE)
 549: t, alive, how = session(store(A, RE + UNMAP_W, val="0x5a")
 550:                         + load(A, RE + UNMAP_R)
 551:                         + store(A, DE + PRIM_START, val="0x02000000")
 552:                         + load(A, 0xffffffff80002000) + ["print v0"])
 553: row("xlatch unm", t.count(declined("0x02000000")), 1,
 554:     "decline fires after BOTH #499 unmapped latches -- kills an alias onto 1<<7 or 1<<8")
 555: row("xlatch unliv", printed(t, "v0") if alive else "died", "0x0",
 556:     "...and the guest RUNS ON through the four-access session")
 557: 
 558: #  *** AND THREE PRE-SET BITS IS THREE CELLS, NOT THE CLASS.  FOUND BY THE
 559: #  PRE-TABLE BLOCK -- the mandatory check I had SKIPPED, run late, which located
 560: #  in one grep what four panels had left. ***
 561: #
 562: #  The block asks: WHO ELSE WRITES THIS FIELD, AND CAN THEY SET MY BIT?  The
 563: #  answer is `grep -c "d->warned |=" dev_sgi_re.c` -> TEN sites, bits 0..9.
 564: #  The rows above pre-set THREE of them (1<<6 via a byte access, 1<<7 and 1<<8
 565: #  via the unmapped windows).  Aliasing SGIRE_W_PRIM_OP onto any of 1<<0..1<<5
 566: #  -- the six *_LEN latches -- escaped every row, for exactly the reason 1<<7
 567: #  did: nothing raised those latches before drawing.
 568: #
 569: #  ONE ROW CLOSES THE WHOLE SPACE, and that is the point.  Every *_LEN latch
 570: #  fires on a wrong length at its own window, so a WORD store to each of the six
 571: #  windows raises bits 0-5; the byte access raises 6; the unmapped write and
 572: #  read raise 7 and 8.  With bits 0-8 all set, an alias onto ANY of them goes
 573: #  silent and this row reds.  Nine cells, one row -- because the closure is over
 574: #  the FIELD, not over the individual bits.
 575: #
 576: #  The narrower rows above are KEPT: they are already measured, they cost
 577: #  nothing, and they say WHICH bit collided, which this row cannot.
 578: #  *** THE FIRST VERSION OF THIS ROW WAS MIS-BUILT, AND THE ENUMERATION SWEEP IS
 579: #  WHAT CAUGHT IT.  M_ALIAS0..M_ALIAS5 each scored a CLEAN 34/34 -- the row was
 580: #  green because NOTHING HAD SET THOSE BITS, which is the vacuous-by-construction
 581: #  shape exactly: a row whose channel cannot vary under its named mutant. ***
 582: #
 583: #  The cause is one line of the device.  DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_re) does
 584: #  `relative_addr += 0x1000;' at dev_sgi_re.c:303, because the device is
 585: #  REGISTERED at `baseaddr + 0x1000' with length 0x1000 -- so memory_rw hands the
 586: #  handler an offset in [0, 0xfff] and the handler adds the window base back.
 587: #
 588: #  CRIME_RE_TLB_A is therefore at DEVICE OFFSET 0x000, not 0x1000:
 589: #
 590: #      window            constant   relative_addr   offset from RE
 591: #      CRIME_RE_TLB_A    0x1000     0x1000          0x000
 592: #      CRIME_RE_TLB_B    0x1200     0x1200          0x200
 593: #      CRIME_RE_TLB_C    0x1400     0x1400          0x400
 594: #      CRIME_RE_TEX      0x1600     0x1600          0x600
 595: #      CRIME_RE_LINEAR_A 0x1700     0x1700          0x700
 596: #      CRIME_RE_LINEAR_B 0x1780     0x1780          0x780
 597: #
 598: #  The first version used the CONSTANTS as offsets, so every one of the six
 599: #  accesses landed at 0x2000..0x2780 -- past the end of a 0x1000-long device, so
 600: #  they reached this device not at all.  `#499's UNMAP_W/UNMAP_R (0x800/0x900)
 601: #  were right for the same reason the others were wrong: they are already
 602: #  device-relative.
 603: #
 604: #  *** THE GENERAL LESSON, AND IT IS THE ONE THIS FILE KEEPS RE-LEARNING: A ROW
 605: #  THAT PASSES PROVES NOTHING UNTIL A MUTANT MAKES IT FAIL.  This row was green
 606: #  on the fixed build, green on every mask mutant, and green on the six alias
 607: #  mutants it was written to kill -- and only the last of those was a defect. ***
 608: A = Addr(CODE)
 609: t, alive, how = session(store(A, RE + 0x000, val="0x5a")       # TLBA_LEN  1<<0
 610:                         + store(A, RE + 0x200, val="0x5a")     # TLBB_LEN  1<<1
 611:                         + store(A, RE + 0x400, val="0x5a")     # TLBC_LEN  1<<2
 612:                         + store(A, RE + 0x600, val="0x5a")     # TEX_LEN   1<<3
 613:                         + store(A, RE + 0x700, val="0x5a")     # LINA_LEN  1<<4
 614:                         + store(A, RE + 0x780, val="0x5a")     # LINB_LEN  1<<5
 615:                         + store(A, DE + 0x0, val="0x5a", op=OP_SB_T0_A3)   # DE_LEN 1<<6
 616:                         + store(A, RE + UNMAP_W, val="0x5a")   # UNMAP_WR  1<<7
 617:                         + load(A, RE + UNMAP_R)                # UNMAP_RD  1<<8
 618:                         + store(A, DE + PRIM_START, val="0x02000000")
 619:                         + store(A, DE + PRIM_START, val="0x02000000")
 620:                         + load(A, 0xffffffff80002000) + ["print v0"])
 621: #  *** SECOND DRAW ADDED: the LATCH-FIELD axis had the same empty 2x2 cell as the
 622: #  operand axis, and a seat flagged it as a softer claim while being right. ***
 623: #
 624: #      NO session both PRE-SET a foreign d->warned bit AND drew twice.
 625: #      xlatch all pre-set bits 0-8 and drew ONCE;  dec flood drew twice and
 626: #      pre-set NOTHING.  So || (d->warned & SGIRE_W_DE_LEN) -- a predicate on the
 627: #      guard's OWN field, squarely inside (d) -- bypassed the latch and flooded,
 628: #      and every row stayed green.
 629: #
 630: #  One extra draw closes it: with the latch working this session still announces
 631: #  ONCE, and under any foreign-bit bypass it announces TWICE.
 632: row("xlatch all", t.count(declined("0x02000000")), 1,
 633:     "decline fires with ALL NINE other latch bits set -- closes the alias space")
 634: row("xlatch alliv", printed(t, "v0") if alive else "died", "0x0",
 635:     "...and the guest RUNS ON through the eleven-access session")
 636: 
 637: #  *** AND THE ROW ABOVE ASSERTS A PRECONDITION THAT NOTHING VERIFIED.  That is
 638: #  how its first version shipped green while touching none of the bits it named. ***
 639: #
 640: #  "decline fires with ALL NINE other latch bits set" is only true if the six
 641: #  window stores actually LAND on their windows.  When they did not, the row was
 642: #  green for the wrong reason -- and stayed green under every mask mutant and
 643: #  under the six alias mutants it existed to kill.  Only M_ALIAS0..5 could see it,
 644: #  and only because the enumeration sweep built them.
 645: #
 646: #  THIS ROW MAKES THE PRECONDITION AN OBSERVABLE.  Each of the six *_LEN guards
 647: #  prints one latched decline, so the session must contain exactly six.  If an
 648: #  offset is ever wrong again, THIS row reds on the FIXED BUILD -- no mutant
 649: #  required, and the failure names itself instead of hiding inside a green row.
 650: #
 651: #  *** THE GENERAL FORM, and it is the cheap half of the vacuity floor: A ROW
 652: #  THAT DEPENDS ON A SET-UP STEP OWES A ROW THAT THE SET-UP STEP HAPPENED. ***
 653: #  *** THIS ORACLE IS AN UNANCHORED PREFIX AND THAT IS DELIBERATE, BUT ITS
 654: #  WARRANT IS NOT IN THE STRING.  Audited after two anchored-substring defects
 655: #  were found in this same file. ***  A count of exactly SIX means six DISTINCT
 656: #  windows only because each *_LEN latch fires ONCE: a session hitting one window
 657: #  twice scores 5, not 6.  So the guarantee rests on the LATCH-ONCE semantics of
 658: #  the device, not on the text.
 659: #
 660: #  *** THE SENTENCE THAT STOOD HERE NAMED A MUTANT THAT CANNOT RED THIS ROW, AND IT
 661: #  IS MEASURED FALSE.  It read: "under M_ASSIGN (the |= dropped) the latch never
 662: #  sets, every access re-emits, and this row reds on a count above 6." ***
 663: #
 664: #  MEASURED across every cell of table 9 -- `got=6 want=6 ok' in ALL 38 logs,
 665: #  M_ASSIGN included.  Two reasons, and both were knowable at writing time:
 666: #
 667: #    * M_ASSIGN's scope is `d->warned |= SGIRE_W_PRIM_OP;' ALONE.  The six *_LEN
 668: #      latches are untouched, so they still fire once each and the count stays 6.
 669: #    * Even a mutant dropping a *_LEN `|=' would not move it: THIS SESSION STORES
 670: #      TO EACH WINDOW EXACTLY ONCE, so one emission per window is all there is to
 671: #      see whether the latch works or not.
 672: #
 673: #  *** SO THIS IS THE CHANNEL CHECK'S OWN FAILURE, FOUND IN THE FILE THAT SHIPPED
 674: #  THE CHANNEL CHECK: a row whose named channel cannot vary under its named mutant
 675: #  is VACUOUS BY CONSTRUCTION, and that is knowable BEFORE any table runs. ***
 676: #
 677: #  THE ROW ITSELF IS KEPT AND IS NOT VACUOUS -- only its stated warrant was wrong.
 678: #  It is a PRECONDITION witness, and its real channel is INSTRUMENT-SIDE: if the
 679: #  six window offsets are ever wrong again the count goes to 0 and this row reds ON
 680: #  THE FIXED BUILD, no mutant required.  That is exactly the defect it was built
 681: #  for, and the one that made `xlatch all' green while touching nothing it named.
 682: #
 683: #  *** "GOES TO 0" IS ITSELF AN UNMEASURED CHANNEL CLAIM, in a block whose whole
 684: #  thesis is that unmeasured channel claims are the defect.  A seat caught it. ***
 685: #  There are SIX distinct emission sites, one per window, so ONE wrong offset gives
 686: #  5, not 0.  It reaches 0 only under a SYSTEMATIC error hitting all six at once --
 687: #  which is exactly what the `+= 0x1000' mistake was.  Right about that historical
 688: #  defect; wrong as a general statement.
 689: #
 690: #  AND THE WITNESS IS 6 OF 9, NOT 9.  `xlatch all' claims "ALL NINE other latch bits
 691: #  set".  This oracle's `sgi_re:' prefix EXCLUDES SGIRE_W_DE_LEN, which prints
 692: #  `sgi_de: unimplemented len=', and NOTHING here witnesses SGIRE_W_UNMAP_WR or _RD.
 693: #  So "closes the alias space" is witnessed at six of nine.  RECORDED, NOT REPAIRED:
 694: #  widening this oracle changes a row that is currently measured and passing, and it
 695: #  belongs in the round that re-tables rather than in a records correction.
 696: #
 697: #  The DEVICE-side mutant that would red it is an alias between two *_LEN latches
 698: #  (give TLBB the TLBA bit: TLBA announces, TLBB is silenced, count 5).  Those
 699: #  latches are #498's and lie OUTSIDE this diff's clause-(d) closure, so that is
 700: #  coverage debt to state, not a floor breach to repair.
 701: #  Do not "anchor" it by appending a window name: that would pin ONE window and
 702: #  lose the distinctness the six is carrying.
 703: row("xlatch pre", t.count("sgi_re: unimplemented len="), 6,
 704:     "the six window stores really landed -- pins the precondition xlatch all needs")
 705: 
 706: # ------------------------------------------------------------- ACCEPT control
 707: #  The de-escalation clause: a fix that removes a host-fatal guard destroys the
 708: #  accidental tripwire that made over-broad declining self-announcing.  So the
 709: #  ACCEPT side is pinned -- an IMPLEMENTED primitive must still be drawn and must
 710: #  NOT be announced as unimplemented.
 711: A = Addr(CODE)
 712: t, alive, how = session(store(A, DE + PRIM_START, val="0x03000000")
 713:                         + load(A, 0xffffffff80002000) + ["print v0"])
 714: row("acc rect", t.count(DRAW), 0,
 715:     "RECTANGLE is IMPLEMENTED -- it must NOT be declined")
 716: row("acc live", printed(t, "v0") if alive else "died", "0x0",
 717:     "...and the guest runs on")
 718: 
 719: #  *** THE ACCEPT ROWS ABOVE PIN THE PREDICATE'S VALUE AND NOT ITS WIDTH, AND A
 720: #  MEASURING SEAT SWEPT THIS DETECTOR 19/19 CLEAN WITH ONE HEX DIGIT:
 721: #  `switch (op & 0xff000000)' -> `& 0xffff0000'. ***
 722: #
 723: #  Both rows above store 0x03000000 -- the low 16 bits are ZERO -- so a mask that
 724: #  grew to cover bits 16-31 still matches them and they stay green.  But
 725: #  crmfbreg.h puts THREE REAL, IMPLEMENTED modifier bits down there:
 726: #
 727: #      DE_PRIM_RL            0x00010000   right to left   read at dev_sgi_re.c:703
 728: #      DE_PRIM_TB            0x00020000   top to bottom   read at            :704
 729: #      DE_PRIM_LINE_SKIP_END 0x00040000   line bound      read at            :735
 730: #
 731: #  So `RECTANGLE | RL' and `LINE | SKIP_END' are LEGAL primitives that the widened
 732: #  mask SILENTLY DECLINES INSTEAD OF DRAWING.
 733: #
 734: #  This is the DE-ESCALATION CLAUSE in its purest form: before #500 that mutant
 735: #  `exit(1)'s on the first right-to-left rectangle -- unmissable.  After #500 it is
 736: #  a silent refusal to draw, and the accidental tripwire that announced it is gone.
 737: #  It is also clause (f)'s accept-side lesson wearing a mask: an accept row at the
 738: #  BASE tests one bound twice and the other never.
 739: #  *** CLASS CLOSURE, NOT ANOTHER CELL.  THIRD PASS, THIRD BREACH OF ONE CLASS. ***
 740: #
 741: #  Pass 2 found the mask width unpinned and I added two rows carrying bit 16 and
 742: #  bit 18.  Pass 3 found that bit 17 was STILL unpinned, so `& 0xff000000' ->
 743: #  `& 0xff020000' -- one hex digit again -- swept the repaired 21 rows CLEAN.
 744: #  Patching the demonstrated cell and leaving the symmetric one is the exact
 745: #  pattern a sibling round spent four passes on, and the rule it minted says:
 746: #  when two consecutive passes find breaches of ONE class, the next repair owes
 747: #  the CLASS CLOSURE -- enumerate the full space from the function's own inputs
 748: #  and close every cell in one diff.
 749: #
 750: #  THE ENUMERATION, DERIVED by grepping every read of `op' in draw_primitive()
 751: #  rather than by listing the bits I happened to know:
 752: #
 753: #      bit 16      DE_PRIM_RL              read at dev_sgi_re.c:703  (dx)
 754: #      bit 17      DE_PRIM_TB              read at             :704  (dy)
 755: #      bit 18      DE_PRIM_LINE_SKIP_END   read at             :735  (loop bound)
 756: #      bits 24-31  the primitive selector  read at             :723  (the switch)
 757: #
 758: #      bits 0-15   DE_PRIM_LINE_WIDTH_MASK -- READ BY NOTHING TODAY.  The only
 759: #                  use is COMMENTED OUT at :729-730.  No row, deliberately: a row
 760: #                  there would assert a reachability the code does not have.
 761: #      bits 19-23  read by nothing at all.  No row, same reason.
 762: #
 763: #  So the LIVE modifier set is exactly bits 16, 17, 18, and each accept row now
 764: #  carries ALL THREE.  Any widening of the switch mask that reaches ANY of them
 765: #  reddens both rows -- the class is closed, not sampled.
 766: #
 767: #  AND THEY NOW HAVE POSITIVE COMPANIONS.  A review seat pointed out that
 768: #  `count(DRAW) == 0' is NEGATIVE-ONLY: it is satisfied when the access never
 769: #  happened at all, so a session that silently did nothing would pass.  Every
 770: #  other survival row in this file is paired; these two were not.  The `* live'
 771: #  rows read "absent" if the guest never got that far.
 772: A = Addr(CODE)
 773: #  *** AND `ALL THREE MODIFIER BITS' WAS STILL SAMPLING.  Three seats, reading
 774: #  independently, found the same hole: bits 16-18 are the three the CODE reads,
 775: #  but the mask can be widened to ANY of bits 0-23, and `& 0xff0000ff' or
 776: #  `& 0xfff00000' left every row green while DECLINING the PROM's own LINE|width.
 777: #
 778: #  The fix is not twenty-four rows.  An accept op with ALL 24 low bits SET is
 779: #  declined under a mask widened at ANY of them, so ONE value closes the whole
 780: #  widening direction:  0x03ffffff & 0xff000000 == RECTANGLE (accepted today),
 781: #  while 0x03ffffff & <any wider mask> is not a case value and reds this row. ***
 782: t, alive, how = session(store(A, DE + PRIM_START, val="0x03ffffff")
 783:                         + load(A, 0xffffffff80002000) + ["print v0"])
 784: row("acc rect mod", t.count(DRAW), 0,
 785:     "RECTANGLE|ALL 24 maskable low bits -- ANY mask widening reds this row")
 786: row("acc rect liv", printed(t, "v0") if alive else "died", "0x0",
 787:     "...and the guest RUNS ON -- the row above is not satisfied by nothing happening")
 788: 
 789: A = Addr(CODE)
 790: t, alive, how = session(store(A, DE + PRIM_START, val="0x01ffffff")
 791:                         + load(A, 0xffffffff80002000) + ["print v0"])
 792: row("acc line mod", t.count(DRAW), 0,
 793:     "LINE|ALL 24 maskable low bits likewise -- both implemented primitives")
 794: row("acc line liv", printed(t, "v0") if alive else "died", "0x0",
 795:     "...and the guest RUNS ON")
 796: 
 797: #  *** THE FOURTH CORNER, AND IT WAS EMPTY.  Found by a seat whose answer had NO
 798: #  VERDICT LINE and was filed as a "format miss" -- the unread-review class, which
 799: #  this project's own record calls its most expensive. ***
 800: #
 801: #  The n25 pair covers two cells of a two-by-two:
 802: #
 803: #                      XFER_EN set        XFER_EN clear
 804: #      op = RECTANGLE  n25 rect  -> 0     (nothing)
 805: #      op = LINE       n25 line  -> 1     *** THIS ROW ***
 806: #
 807: #  Nowhere was (LINE, no-XFER) -> 0 asserted, so dropping the guard entirely --
 808: #  `if (drawmode & DE_DRAWMODE_XFER_EN)' -> `if (1)', ONE TOKEN, SMALLER than the
 809: #  conjunct already disclosed -- left every row green: the LINE arm is never
 810: #  entered in the rectangle session, `n25 line' still fires exactly once, and
 811: #  `acc line mod' draws a LINE but only ever counts the DECLINE string.
 812: #
 813: #  *** IT COSTS NO NEW SESSION.  The `acc line mod' session above already draws a
 814: #  LINE with drawmode CLEAR, so the missing assertion is one count on a transcript
 815: #  this file has been collecting all along. ***  That is why this is CLOSED rather
 816: #  than disclosed: unlike the conjunct and the op-keyed guard, killing it needs no
 817: #  channel that observes which primitive ran.
 818: row("n25 nox", t.count(XFER_LINE), 0,
 819:     "a LINE draw with drawmode CLEAR must stay silent -- kills a dropped guard")
 820: 
 821: #  *** AND `n25 nox' HAD THE SAME DEFECT AS THE ROW ABOVE, ONE SESSION OVER --
 822: #  found by a second seat in the same pass.  Its claim is "a LINE draw with
 823: #  drawmode CLEAR"; nothing pinned that the op WAS a line or that drawmode WAS
 824: #  clear.  Swap this session's op to RECT and the row still counts zero -- not
 825: #  because the guard holds, but because there is no LINE to diagnose. ***
 826: #
 827: #  Chosen by DUMPING this session rather than reasoning: it announces
 828: #  `op = 0x01ffffff, drawmode=0x0 src_mode=', which pins BOTH facts in one
 829: #  anchored string that cannot extend.
 830: row("n25 noxset", t.count("op = 0x01ffffff, drawmode=0x0 src_mode="), 1,
 831:     "...and THIS session really was a LINE with drawmode CLEAR -- pins n25 nox")
 832: 
 833: #  *** AND THE MASK HAS TWO FAILURE DIRECTIONS, NOT ONE -- AND MY FIRST ATTEMPT AT
 834: #  THE SECOND ONE SAMPLED IT INSTEAD OF CLOSING IT.  FOURTH BREACH, SAME CLASS. ***
 835: #
 836: #  The switch reads bits 24-31.  The implemented values are 0x01 and 0x03, so bits
 837: #  26-31 must be ZERO for an op to be implemented.  A mask NARROWED by clearing any
 838: #  one of those bits makes an unimplemented op ALIAS ONTO an implemented case and be
 839: #  SILENTLY DRAWN instead of declined -- which is worse than wrongly refusing.
 840: #
 841: #  ATTEMPT 1 used ONE op, 0x05000000, which carries bit 26 alone.  A review seat
 842: #  found the hole immediately: `& 0xff000000' -> `& 0xf7000000' clears bit 27, that
 843: #  op still declines, the row stays GREEN, and 0x09000000 gets drawn as LINE with
 844: #  nothing to see it.  I had written "the mask has two failure directions" in this
 845: #  very file, closed the widening one with ALL THREE modifier bits, and then pinned
 846: #  the narrowing one at a SINGLE bit.
 847: #
 848: #  *** THE SPACE IS FINITE AND SMALL, SO IT IS ENUMERATED, NOT SAMPLED. ***  Six
 849: #  bits must be zero for an implemented op, so six ops discriminate them -- each is
 850: #  LINE with exactly one high bit added, so clearing THAT bit from the mask aliases
 851: #  it onto LINE and clearing any other bit does not:
 852: #
 853: #      bit 26  0x05000000     bit 29  0x21000000
 854: #      bit 27  0x09000000     bit 30  0x41000000
 855: #      bit 28  0x11000000     bit 31  0x81000000
 856: #
 857: #  Six rows is not cell-by-cell repair: it is the COMPLETE enumeration of a
 858: #  six-element space, which is what closure means when the space is finite.
 859: #  *** AND THESE SIX WERE SINGLE-STORE, WHICH MADE THEM STRUCTURALLY BLIND TO THE
 860: #  ENTIRE LATCH-BYPASS FAMILY.  MEASURED CLEAN SWEEPS -- floor clause (d), L2. ***
 861: #
 862: #  A measuring seat built the mutants and scored them against the SHIPPED 47-row
 863: #  detector (its logs, all ending OPUS500_DONE, are in this round's artifacts):
 864: #
 865: #      M_FLOOD_B25   latch || (op & 0x02000000)   44/47   two once, xlatch all, dec flood
 866: #      M_FLOOD_B26   latch || (op & 0x04000000)   46/47   dec ops
 867: #      M_FLOOD_B27   latch || (op & 0x08000000)   *** 47/47 TOTAL ESCAPE ***
 868: #      M_FLOOD_B31   latch || (op & 0x80000000)   *** 47/47 TOTAL ESCAPE ***
 869: #
 870: #  *** MY CRITERION WAS WRONG, AND IT WAS WRONG IN THE ANSWER I HAD JUST WRITTEN
 871: #  ONE PASS EARLIER. ***  I claimed no green control could exist because every bit
 872: #  is carried by SOME decline op.  The union claim is TRUE -- and it is not the
 873: #  criterion.  A latch bypass is only observable on a SECOND announcement, so a
 874: #  row can red only if its session reaches `default:' at least twice.  There were
 875: #  exactly three such sessions:
 876: #
 877: #  *** "OF THE SAME OP LITERAL" STOOD HERE AND IS STRUCK.  Pass 13: `two once'
 878: #  stores 0x00000000 then 0x02000000 -- two DIFFERENT literals -- and observes a
 879: #  bypass through the DRAW prefix.  The correct criterion, from the measuring seat:
 880: #  a bypass is observable iff some session reaches `default:' at least twice with
 881: #  the predicate TRUE on a later reach.  That reformulation asks about ALL state at
 882: #  the second reach, not just `op', and it is what exposed the register family. ***
 883: #
 884: #      two once / xlatch all   op 0x02000000   observes bit 25
 885: #      dec ops                 op 0x04000000   observes bit 26
 886: #      dec flood               op 0x02ffffff   observes bits 0-23 and 25
 887: #
 888: #  Observable: 0-23, 25, 26.  OPEN: 24, 27, 28, 29, 30, 31.  **`dec b26' stores
 889: #  0x05000000 -- which IS bit 26 -- and stayed GREEN under M_FLOOD_B26**, which is
 890: #  the proof that CARRYING a bit is not OBSERVING it.  I cited these six rows as the
 891: #  reason no green control exists; they are six rows that cannot red.
 892: #
 893: #  *** AND IT IS NOT A HARMLESS BLIND SPOT.  A bypass on bit 27 makes the device
 894: #  re-announce on EVERY access to op 0x09000000, a guest-writable value -- the exact
 895: #  log flood the `(once)' latch exists to prevent, and a class the CATCHES contract
 896: #  above names.  There is no bit position that is both un-observable and harmless. ***
 897: #
 898: #  THIRD GENERATION OF ONE CLASS (TB -> B26 -> B27), so the carrier's amendment
 899: #  governs: the next repair owes the CLASS CLOSURE, not another cell.
 900: #
 901: #  *** THE CLOSURE IS ONE LINE AND ADDS NO ROWS: DRAW TWICE. ***  Every op here is
 902: #  LINE|bit_N, so each carries bit 24 AND its own high bit.  Two stores of the same
 903: #  literal with `want=1' means a bypass on bit 24 or on ANY of bits 26-31 announces
 904: #  twice and reds that row.  With `dec flood' covering 0-23 and 25, the 32-bit space
 905: #  is CLOSED rather than sampled -- and the rows additionally strengthen from "MUST
 906: #  decline" to "MUST decline EXACTLY ONCE".
 907: #
 908: #  *** THE BYPASS HALF IS MEASURED AT ALL SIX BITS (table 11).  THE NARROWING HALF IS
 909: #  MEASURED AT 26, 27 AND 31 ONLY -- M_NARROW_28/29/30 were never built, in any
 910: #  table since pass 5.  The mechanism is uniform (one row red, count 0, at each
 911: #  measured bit) so the derivation is believed; but a row note that said "narrowing
 912: #  AND bypass" claimed six measurements where there are three.  A seat caught it in
 913: #  the pass that had just diagnosed a closure "verifying two of six". ***
 914: for _bit, _op in ((26, "0x05000000"), (27, "0x09000000"), (28, "0x11000000"),
 915:                   (29, "0x21000000"), (30, "0x41000000"), (31, "0x81000000")):
 916:     A = Addr(CODE)
 917:     t, alive, how = session(store(A, DE + PRIM_START, val=_op)
 918:                             + store(A, DE + PRIM_START, val=_op)
 919:                             + load(A, 0xffffffff80002000) + ["print v0"])
 920:     row("dec b%d" % _bit, t.count(declined(_op)), 1,
 921:         "%s = LINE|bit%d declines ONCE in TWO draws -- bypass at bit %d (narrowing: see note)"
 922:         % (_op, _bit, _bit))
 923: 
 924: #  ONE liveness companion for the family rather than six: the six rows above differ
 925: #  only in which bit they carry, and a host death in any of them would show as a
 926: #  missing message in that row.  This proves the shape of session they all use does
 927: #  reach the tail and run on.
 928: A = Addr(CODE)
 929: t, alive, how = session(store(A, DE + PRIM_START, val="0x81000000")
 930:                         + load(A, 0xffffffff80002000) + ["print v0"])
 931: row("dec hi liv", printed(t, "v0") if alive else "died", "0x0",
 932:     "...and the guest RUNS ON after the highest-bit decline")
 933: 
 934: #  *** THE DECLINE SIDE CARRIED NO LOW BITS AT ALL, AND THE ACCEPT SIDE HAS
 935: #  CARRIED ALL 24 SINCE PASS 4.  A seat found the asymmetry by building the
 936: #  mutant it admits.  L2, clause (d), repaired here. ***
 937: #
 938: #  THE MUTANT: qualify this round's OWN latch predicate with a bit of `op' --
 939: #
 940: #      if (!(d->warned & SGIRE_W_PRIM_OP) &&
 941: #          !(op & DE_PRIM_RL)) {
 942: #
 943: #  -- which reads as an innocuous extra condition.  **Every op this file sends to
 944: #  `default:' has bit 16 CLEAR**, so the guard is unchanged for all of them and
 945: #  the table scores 41/41.  Meanwhile a real `DE_PRIM_TRIANGLE | DE_PRIM_RL'
 946: #  (0x02010000) reaches `default:' and is SILENTLY DECLINED -- the announcement
 947: #  this round exists to make is gone, for exactly the ops a guest sets modifiers
 948: #  on.
 949: #
 950: #  *** THE ASYMMETRY IS THE FINDING, NOT THE BIT.  `acc rect mod' and `acc line
 951: #  mod' have carried 0x??ffffff since pass 4 precisely so any mask WIDENING reds
 952: #  them.  The decline ops were 0x05000000, 0x09000000 ... 0x81000000 and bare
 953: #  0x02000000 -- not one low bit set among them.  I closed that direction on the
 954: #  accept side and left its mirror open on the decline side for four passes. ***
 955: #
 956: #  SO THIS IS ONE ROW, NOT A ROW PER BIT: an op carrying ALL 24 maskable low bits
 957: #  reds under a qualification on ANY of them.  0x02ffffff & 0xff000000 is
 958: #  0x02000000, still `default:', still declined -- and every low bit is set, so
 959: #  `&& !(op & <anything>)' nulls the announce and this row sees it.
 960: A = Addr(CODE)
 961: t, alive, how = session(store(A, DE + PRIM_START, val="0x02ffffff")
 962:                         + load(A, 0xffffffff80002000) + ["print v0"])
 963: row("dec low", t.count(declined("0x02ffffff")), 1,
 964:     "a DECLINED op carrying ALL 24 low bits -- kills a guard qualified on any of them")
 965: row("dec low liv", printed(t, "v0") if alive else "died", "0x0",
 966:     "...and the guest RUNS ON")
 967: 
 968: #  *** AND THE OTHER SEVEN OPERANDS WERE ZERO IN EVERY DECLINE SESSION.  Found by
 969: #  a seat answering the question this brief actually asked -- WHICH SYMMETRY IS
 970: #  STILL HALF-CLOSED. ***
 971: #
 972: #  The latch's guarded body reads EIGHT values: `op' and the seven the fatal()
 973: #  prints -- x1, y1, x2, y2, fg, bg, pattern.  Clause (d) admits an operand swap
 974: #  drawn from any field the guarded body reads, so:
 975: #
 976: #      if (!(d->warned & SGIRE_W_PRIM_OP) && !x1) {
 977: #
 978: #  is inside the enumeration -- and **every decline session in this file left all
 979: #  seven at ZERO**, as its own `declined()' literal records (`x1=0 y1=0 ...').
 980: #  So `!x1' is true for every row, the guard is unchanged, and the table sweeps
 981: #  clean -- while any real draw with a non-zero vertex loses its announcement.
 982: #
 983: #  *** ONE SESSION SETS ALL SEVEN, NOT ONE ROW PER OPERAND.  The seat that found
 984: #  this also named how I would get the repair wrong: "do not pin only x1 -- that
 985: #  would be pass 2-3 / 6-7 again; a row that only sets x1=1 leaves && !fg as pass
 986: #  10."  That is this round's own failure shape, called in advance rather than
 987: #  discovered next pass. ***
 988: #
 989: #  PATTERN NEEDS BIT 31 SPECIFICALLY, and the same seat supplied the reason: at
 990: #  reset `stipple_mode' is 0, so the strip chain is `>>= 31; <<= 31; <<= 0' and
 991: #  keeps ONLY bit 31.  A naive non-zero stipple pattern still prints 0x00000000.
 992: #  *** AND THE OPERAND AXIS WAS A 2x2 WITH ONE CELL EMPTY.  Eleventh defect, and
 993: #  a seat stated it as the grid rather than as a mutant. ***
 994: #
 995: #                     ONE draw            TWO draws
 996: #      zero operands  (older rows)        dec flood
 997: #      NONZERO        dec ops             *** EMPTY ***
 998: #
 999: #  The empty cell is `|| x1' -- FLOOD keyed on a NON-ZERO body operand.  Under it
1000: #  `dec ops' (x1=17, one draw) bypasses the latch and still announces ONCE, so it
1001: #  stays green; `dec flood' (x1=0, two draws) has `|| x1' FALSE so the latch works
1002: #  normally and also stays green.  It swept 47/47.
1003: #
1004: #  ONE CHANGE COVERS THE WHOLE AXIS: make this a TWO-DRAW session.  Then
1005: #  `&& !x1' gives ZERO announces and `|| x1' gives TWO -- both red this row, and
1006: #  the silence/flood directions of the operand axis are closed together rather
1007: #  than one now and its mirror in pass 12.
1008: #
1009: #  *** THE OPERAND VALUES ALSO EXERCISE THE DECODE MASKS NOW, and that part is
1010: #  OUTSIDE clause (d) -- the same seat said so and declined to hang L2 on it.
1011: #  `y1 = v & 0x7ff' with the old 0x0022 could not see a mask widened to 0xfff,
1012: #  because 0x22 has no high bits.  0xFCBA & 0x7ff = 0x2BA while & 0xfff = 0xCBA,
1013: #  so the widening now shows.  Taken as a free strengthening, labelled as such,
1014: #  not as a floor repair. ***
1015: _ops_op = "0x04000000"
1016: #  *** y1 COMPUTED BY HAND AS 698 AND IT IS 1210.  0xfcba & 0x7ff = 0x4ba.
1017: #  Caught by running the arithmetic BEFORE applying the edit rather than after --
1018: #  the literal would have been false and the row would have read 0. ***
1019: _ops_lit = ("UNIMPLEMENTED drawing op = %s, x1=17 y1=1210 x2=51 y2=1483"
1020:             " fg=0x55 bg=0x66 pattern=0x80000000 -- declined.  (once) ]" % _ops_op)
1021: A = Addr(CODE)
1022: t, alive, how = session(store(A, DE + VERTEX_0, val="0x0811fcba")
1023:                         + store(A, DE + VERTEX_1, val="0x0833fdcb")
1024:                         + store(A, DE + DE_FG, val="0x55")
1025:                         + store(A, DE + DE_BG, val="0x66")
1026:                         + store(A, DE + STIPPLE_PAT, val="0x80000000")
1027:                         + store(A, DE + PRIM_START, val=_ops_op)
1028:                         + store(A, DE + PRIM_START, val=_ops_op)
1029:                         + load(A, 0xffffffff80002000) + ["print v0"])
1030: row("dec ops", t.count(_ops_lit), 1,
1031:     "SEVEN non-zero operands, TWO draws -- kills BOTH && !operand and || operand")
1032: row("dec ops liv", printed(t, "v0") if alive else "died", "0x0",
1033:     "...and the guest RUNS ON through both draws of the seven-store session")
1034: 
1035: #  *** AND THE SILENCE/FLOOD SYMMETRY WAS HALF-CLOSED TOO -- a SECOND seat, in the
1036: #  same pass, on a different axis from the first. ***
1037: #
1038: #  `dec low' closes conditional SILENCE:   && !(op & low_bit)   -- the guard is
1039: #  qualified so the announce is skipped.  The MIRROR is conditional FLOODING:
1040: #
1041: #      if (!(d->warned & SGIRE_W_PRIM_OP) || (op & DE_PRIM_RL)) {
1042: #
1043: #  where a low-bit op BYPASSES the latch and announces on EVERY draw instead of
1044: #  once.  That swept all 43 rows, and the reason is structural: **every low-bit
1045: #  decline session in this file performs exactly ONE draw**, so one message is all
1046: #  there is to see either way.  The only repeated-draw row, `two once', uses
1047: #  0x00000000 and 0x02000000 -- every low bit CLEAR -- so its second announcement
1048: #  stays correctly suppressed and the mutant is invisible to it.
1049: #
1050: #  *** THIS IS THE SAME SYMMETRY HABIT ONE LEVEL DOWN: pass 8 closed the silence
1051: #  direction on low bits and left the flood direction on the same bits untested.
1052: #  `two once' pins the latch for ops WITHOUT low bits; nothing pinned it for ops
1053: #  WITH them. ***
1054: #
1055: #  The row is `two once' with a low-bit op: two draws of the SAME declined value
1056: #  in one session, and exactly ONE announcement.  Under the flood mutant it sees
1057: #  two.
1058: #  *** THE OP WAS 0x02010000 AND THE DISCLOSURE THAT JUSTIFIED IT WAS A
1059: #  RATIONALISATION.  Tenth defect, tenth pass, and it is mine. ***
1060: #
1061: #  I wrote that full flood coverage was structurally impossible in one session --
1062: #  "the bypass must be on a bit the REPEATED-DRAW op carries, so covering all 24
1063: #  would need 24 two-draw sessions" -- and disclosed the gap on that basis.
1064: #
1065: #  A seat pointed out that `dec low' ALREADY uses 0x02ffffff, which carries ALL
1066: #  24 low bits.  Put THAT op in the two-draw session and a bypass on ANY low bit
1067: #  makes it announce twice.  ONE session covers all 24; the 24-session claim is
1068: #  false.
1069: #
1070: #  *** IT IS THE IDENTICAL TRICK I USED ON THE SILENCE SIDE IN PASS 8, AND I
1071: #  EXPLICITLY WROTE THAT IT COULD NOT BE USED HERE.  A disclosure with a false
1072: #  reason is worse than an open hole: it tells the next reader the gap is
1073: #  unclosable when it is one constant away from closed. ***
1074: _fl_op = "0x02ffffff"
1075: _fl_lit = ("UNIMPLEMENTED drawing op = %s, x1=0 y1=0 x2=0 y2=0"
1076:            " fg=0x0 bg=0x0 pattern=0x00000000 -- declined.  (once) ]" % _fl_op)
1077: A = Addr(CODE)
1078: t, alive, how = session(store(A, DE + PRIM_START, val=_fl_op)
1079:                         + store(A, DE + PRIM_START, val=_fl_op)
1080:                         + load(A, 0xffffffff80002000) + ["print v0"])
1081: row("dec flood", t.count(_fl_lit), 1,
1082:     "TWO draws of an ALL-LOW-BIT declined op announce ONCE -- kills a bypass on ANY of the 24")
1083: row("dec flood liv", printed(t, "v0") if alive else "died", "0x0",
1084:     "...and the guest RUNS ON through both draws")
1085: 
1086: #  *** PASS 13 MEASURED FOUR MORE CLEAN SWEEPS OF THE SHIPPED DETECTOR, AND TWO OF
1087: #  THEM ARE A FAMILY THAT ONE SESSION CLOSES.  L2, clause (d), third consecutive
1088: #  pass -- the class-closure amendment governs. ***
1089: #
1090: #      M_XFER      latch || (drawmode & DE_DRAWMODE_XFER_EN)   47/47  TOTAL ESCAPE
1091: #      M_SRCMODE   latch || (src_mode & 0x00001c00)            47/47  TOTAL ESCAPE
1092: #      M_OPEQ      latch || (op == 0x07000000)                 47/47  (see contract)
1093: #      M_FG        latch || (fg & 0x80)                        47/47  (see contract)
1094: #
1095: #  THE FAMILY: draw_primitive() reads ELEVEN registers.  On every path that reached
1096: #  `default:' in this file, FIVE of them were identically ZERO -- drawmode, src_mode,
1097: #  dst_mode, rop, stipple_mode -- because the only sessions that wrote any of them
1098: #  (`n25 rect', `n25 line') use IMPLEMENTED ops that never reach `default:'.  So a
1099: #  bypass on any nonzero bit of any of the five was invisible to all 47 rows, and
1100: #  XFER_EN is a bit real blit code sets: a guest-reachable flood on an ordinary path,
1101: #  which is the class the CATCHES contract names.
1102: #
1103: #  *** HOW THE BRIEF'S OWN SCOPING HID IT: it asked for predicates on values "the
1104: #  guarded BODY reads".  drawmode is read by the enclosing FUNCTION, three times,
1105: #  above the switch.  Whether that is inside clause (d)'s DEPENDS-ON bound is a RULE
1106: #  question filed for adjudication: the bound says "the guarded body", but the
1107: #  carrier's own exemplar of a qualified condition uses `writeflag', which is
1108: #  likewise an enclosing-function value.  The detector is repaired REGARDLESS of how
1109: #  that resolves, because the repair is cheap and the flood is real. ***
1110: #
1111: #  ONE SESSION CLOSES THE FAMILY AND THE RESET-VALUE EQUALITY TOGETHER: set all five
1112: #  registers to all-ones, then draw POINT twice.  Every register bit-test fires on
1113: #  the second reach, and so does `|| (op == 0x00000000)' -- the one equality value a
1114: #  guest reaches with NO register write, and this round's own headline case, which
1115: #  six seats independently named as a clean sweep.  drawmode=0xffffffff also sets
1116: #  XFER_EN, whose block above the switch prints an "unimplemented XFER" line with its
1117: #  exit(1) COMMENTED OUT, and stipple_mode=0xffffffff trips an UNLATCHED fatal() --
1118: #  both non-fatal, both a different string from the one this row counts.  (Those two
1119: #  unlatched guest-drivable fatal()s are pre-existing and filed, not this round's.)
1120: #
1121: #  The literal is unchanged: x1..pattern come from registers this session leaves at
1122: #  zero, and stipple_mode all-ones strips pattern to bit 31 of zero, which is zero.
1123: A = Addr(CODE)
1124: t, alive, how = session(store(A, DE + MODE_SRC, val="0xffffffff")
1125:                         + store(A, DE + MODE_DST, val="0xffffffff")
1126:                         + store(A, DE + DRAWMODE, val="0xffffffff")
1127:                         + store(A, DE + STIPPLE_MODE, val="0xffffffff")
1128:                         + store(A, DE + DE_ROP, val="0xffffffff")
1129:                         + store(A, DE + XFER_ADDR_SRC, val="0xffffffff")
1130:                         + store(A, DE + XFER_STRD_SRC, val="0xffffffff")
1131:                         + store(A, DE + XFER_STEP_X, val="0xffffffff")
1132:                         + store(A, DE + XFER_STEP_Y, val="0xffffffff")
1133:                         + store(A, DE + XFER_ADDR_DST, val="0xffffffff")
1134:                         + store(A, DE + XFER_STRD_DST, val="0xffffffff")
1135:                         + store(A, DE + PRIM_START, val="0x00000000")
1136:                         + store(A, DE + PRIM_START, val="0x00000000")
1137:                         + load(A, 0xffffffff80002000) + ["print v0"])
1138: #  *** PASS 15: THE PASS-14 CLOSURE OPENED SIX MORE ZERO REGISTERS, AND TWO READERS
1139: #  PREDICTED IT.  MEASURED: `|| step_x' swept 50/50 (artifacts/sgideprimop/gapB_20260903/).
1140: #  Setting drawmode all-ones sets XFER_EN, and the XFER block above the switch then reads
1141: #  addr_src, strd_src, step_x, step_y, addr_dst, strd_dst -- all zero in every session
1142: #  until now.  Third instance of one family; the amendment says close the SPACE: every
1143: #  register draw_primitive() reads on a decline path is now all-ones here.  ELEVEN
1144: #  registers.  The XFER block's only exit is commented out, so all-ones is safe. ***
1145: row("dec regs", t.count(declined("0x00000000")), 1,
1146:     "ELEVEN all-ones registers, POINT twice, ONE announce -- kills any register-bit or op==0 bypass")
1147: row("dec regs liv", printed(t, "v0") if alive else "died", "0x0",
1148:     "...and the guest RUNS ON through the thirteen-store session")
1149: #  *** AND THE ROW ABOVE OWES A PRECONDITION WITNESS, the lesson `xlatch all' paid
1150: #  for: "five all-ones registers" is only true if the five stores LAND.  The entry
1151: #  announcement prints drawmode and src_mode verbatim, so pin both, anchored on the
1152: #  field that follows so a wider value cannot satisfy it. ***
1153: #  *** want=2, NOT 1: the entry announcement prints ONCE PER DRAW and this session
1154: #  draws TWICE.  The first version wanted 1 -- copied from `n25 rectset', a one-draw
1155: #  session -- and BASE measured 49/50 with THIS row red on the fixed build.  That is
1156: #  the canary working: a precondition witness whose want does not match the session
1157: #  shape reds on BASE before any mutant is scored.  Pass 14, table 12. ***
1158: #  *** PASS 15: the anchor now pins dst_mode's VALUE too (three of the eleven).  Two seats
1159: #  found the old anchor certified nothing past src_mode while the note said "really landed".
1160: #  rop and the six XFER registers have NO channel in THIS line; stipple_mode has its own
1161: #  (next row).  So the precondition is witnessed at 4 of 11 by standing rows.
1162: #  *** PASS 16: THE SIX XFER REGISTERS DO HAVE A CHANNEL -- the XFER block's own
1163: #  unlatched fatal() prints all six by name once per draw, and under this session it
1164: #  fires (step_x = -1 != src_bufdepth 8).  One want=2 row would take the standing
1165: #  witness to 10 of 11.  FILED as sgideprimopstrengthen with the latch-field mutant
1166: #  and the `rop & 1' cell, because the chain closed on this pass and a new row is a
1167: #  new floor-bound diff.  Today the seven rest on the table-13 kills. ***
1168: row("dec regs set", t.count("op = 0x00000000, drawmode=0xffffffff src_mode=0xffffffff dst_mode=0xffffffff"), 2,
1169:     "drawmode/src_mode/dst_mode stores landed, both draws -- 3 of 11 pinned here, 1 more below")
1170: #  stipple_mode all-ones trips the UNLATCHED pre-switch fatal(), which PRINTS THE VALUE --
1171: #  a channel for the fourth register, once per draw.  (That fatal is a pre-existing
1172: #  guest-drivable flood, filed; here it is used as a witness, not endorsed.)
1173: row("dec regs stip", t.count("UNIMPLEMENTED stipple_mode bits: 0xffffffff"), 2,
1174:     "...and stipple_mode all-ones landed, seen on BOTH draws -- 4 of 11 witnessed")
1175: 
1176: #  *** WHAT dec flood COVERS, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT -- measured in BOTH directions,
1177: #  which is the half I kept skipping.  ***
1178: #  (This line cited `negctl_dec_flood.out'.  NO SUCH FILE: the artifact is the
1179: #  SCRIPT `killtable_20260902_table9/negctl_dec_flood.sh' and its measurements are
1180: #  `decflood_M_FLOOD_{RL,TB}.out' and `flood2_M_FLOOD_{B26,SK,TB}.out' in that dir.
1181: #  A bare filename evades the cited-artifact checker.  Found by a seat, pass 13.)
1182: #
1183: #      M_FLOOD_RL   (op & DE_PRIM_RL)   bit 16 -> RED    dec flood  (op carries it)
1184: #      M_FLOOD_TB   (op & DE_PRIM_TB)   bit 17 -> RED    dec flood  (carries it too, now)
1185: #      M_FLOOD_SK   (op & SKIP_END)     bit 18 -> RED    dec flood
1186: #      M_FLOOD_B26  (op & 0x04000000)   bit 26 -> *** REDS `dec ops', NOT green ***
1187: #
1188: #  *** THE B26 CELL WAS PREDICTED 47/47 AND MEASURED 46/47.  Table 10,
1189: #  killtable_20260902_table10/M_FLOOD_B26.out.  The prediction was written by
1190: #  reasoning about `dec flood' ALONE and applied to the WHOLE TABLE. ***
1191: #
1192: #  `dec ops' stores op = 0x04000000, which IS bit 26.  So the bypass fires in THAT
1193: #  session, and since pass 10 made it a TWO-DRAW session it announces twice and the
1194: #  row reds.  `dec flood' behaved exactly as claimed and stayed green.
1195: #
1196: #  *** SO THE ROW-LEVEL CLAIM SURVIVED AND THE CELL-LEVEL ONE DID NOT, and that
1197: #  distinction is the finding.  A "deliberately GREEN" control is a claim about
1198: #  EVERY row in the table, not about the row it was designed for -- it asserts that
1199: #  NOTHING responds to a bypass on a bit the op lacks.  Checking it against one row
1200: #  is checking a universal by looking at one case. ***
1201: #
1202: #  THIRD GENERATION OF THIS EXACT SLIP, which is why it is written out rather than
1203: #  just patched: the green control was TB until pass 10 widened `_fl_op' and made TB
1204: #  legitimately red; it moved to B26; and B26 is not green either, for a DIFFERENT
1205: #  reason -- a different row's op.  The cheap check that would have caught all three
1206: #  is one grep: **before calling a bypass-bit control green, test the bit against
1207: #  EVERY op literal in the file, not against the one row you have in mind.**
1208: #
1209: #  Bits 26-31 are carried by the six `dec b*' ops (each is LINE|bit_N) and by
1210: #  `dec ops' (0x04000000).  There is therefore NO high bit that is absent from every
1211: #  decline op, so a green control of this shape may not exist at all here -- which is
1212: #  worth stating rather than hunting for one.  M_OPS_ROP remains a MEASURED green
1213: #  (47/47): `rop' is read by the enclosing function and not by the latch body, so it
1214: #  is outside what any row claims.
1215: #
1216: #  *** THE GREEN CONTROL HAD TO MOVE.  It was TB, and with the op widened to
1217: #  0x02ffffff the row legitimately reds on TB -- so leaving the control there
1218: #  would have quietly turned the honest half of it vacuous.  Bit 26 is clear in
1219: #  0x02ffffff, so it is the bit that still proves this row keys on what its own
1220: #  op carries rather than on any bypass whatever. ***
1221: #
1222: #  *** THE PARAGRAPH THAT STOOD HERE WAS STALE THE MOMENT THE OP WIDENED, AND IT
1223: #  SAT THREE LINES BELOW ITS OWN CORRECTION READING AS LIVE EXPLANATION.  It said:
1224: #  "this row keys on the bit its OWN op carries (0x02010000 has bit 16 set, bit 17
1225: #  clear)".  `_fl_op' is 0x02ffffff and has bit 17 SET -- so the sentence
1226: #  contradicted the corrected table directly above it, which records TB as RED. ***
1227: #
1228: #  This is the carrier's own rule turned on this file: WHEN A CLAIM IS SUPERSEDED,
1229: #  CORRECT ITS CONCLUSION -- do not leave it standing below the correction, where
1230: #  it still reads as the verdict.  Pass 10 widened the op and rewrote the table;
1231: #  the prose UNDER the table was not re-read.
1232: #
1233: #  THE CLAIM, RESTATED FOR THE OP THAT IS ACTUALLY THERE, AND THEN CORRECTED AGAIN
1234: #  BY THE TABLE THAT FOLLOWED IT: `0x02ffffff' carries every one of the 24 low bits
1235: #  and NOT bit 26, so a bit-26 bypass cannot reach THIS row, and measurement agrees
1236: #  -- `dec flood' is green under M_FLOOD_B26.
1237: #
1238: #  *** BUT THE CELL IS NOT GREEN: M_FLOOD_B26 measured 46/47, redding `dec ops',
1239: #  whose own op IS 0x04000000.  The restatement above was written in pass 11 and
1240: #  falsified by table 10 the same evening -- see the corrected table at the
1241: #  M_FLOOD_* block.  A per-row claim was again dressed as a per-table one. ***
1242: #
1243: #  *** THE PARAGRAPH THAT STOOD HERE CLAIMED THIS ASYMMETRY WAS STRUCTURAL AND IT
1244: #  WAS NOT.  Kept in substance because the correction is the point: it argued the
1245: #  flood direction "cannot use that trick", that "the bypass must be on a bit the
1246: #  REPEATED-DRAW op carries", and that covering all 24 "would need 24 two-draw
1247: #  sessions".  Every clause is true EXCEPT the conclusion -- because the
1248: #  repeated-draw op can simply BE the all-bits op.  The coverage is now equal to
1249: #  dec low's, not narrower. ***
1250: 
1251: #  *** THE `DISCLOSED, NOT COVERED' CLAUSE FOR BITS 24-25 WAS A WRONG RECORD, AND
1252: #  A REVIEW SEAT REFUTED IT BY READING THE PRODUCT CODE.  L4 + L2. ***
1253: #
1254: #  I had recorded a mask narrowing at bit 24 or 25 as UNCOVERABLE: it collapses
1255: #  the two IMPLEMENTED cases onto each other rather than restoring a host kill,
1256: #  and seeing a wrong DRAW needs a pixel oracle, which does not exist at reset
1257: #  (horrible_getputpixel returns early on a zero TLB pointer).  All of that is
1258: #  true and NONE of it supports the conclusion.
1259: #
1260: #  `case DE_PRIM_LINE:' opens with a diagnostic that exists in THAT ARM AND
1261: #  NOWHERE ELSE:
1262: #
1263: #      case DE_PRIM_LINE:
1264: #              if (drawmode & DE_DRAWMODE_XFER_EN)
1265: #                      fatal("[ sgi_de: XFER_EN for LINE op? ]\n");
1266: #
1267: #  So the two primitives ARE distinguishable, with no pixel oracle at all: set
1268: #  XFER_EN, draw a RECTANGLE, and the message must NOT appear.  Under
1269: #  `& 0xfd000000' the RECTANGLE folds onto LINE and it does.
1270: #
1271: #  *** TWO ROWS, NOT ONE, AND THIS IS THE ROUND'S OWN LESSON APPLIED THREE HOURS
1272: #  LATE.  `n25 rect' asserts a count of ZERO, which is NEGATIVE-ONLY: it is
1273: #  satisfied if the channel is dead for ANY reason -- a wrong register offset, a
1274: #  guard I misread, a drawmode that never lands.  `n25 line' proves the SAME
1275: #  channel does fire.  Earlier in this round `xlatch all' shipped green because
1276: #  its set-up silently did nothing, and only a nine-mutant sweep caught it. ***
1277: A = Addr(CODE)
1278: t, alive, how = session(store(A, DE + DRAWMODE, val=XFER_EN)
1279:                         + store(A, DE + PRIM_START, val="0x03000000")
1280:                         + load(A, 0xffffffff80002000) + ["print v0"])
1281: row("n25 rect", t.count(XFER_LINE), 0,
1282:     "RECTANGLE must NOT reach the LINE-only diagnostic -- kills a bit-25 narrowing")
1283: row("n25 rectliv", printed(t, "v0") if alive else "died", "0x0",
1284:     "...and the guest RUNS ON through the rectangle session")
1285: 
1286: #  *** THE PAIR HAD NO IN-SESSION WITNESS, AND A SEAT BUILT THE CLEAN SWEEP THAT
1287: #  PROVES IT.  L2, floor clause (d), repaired here.  THIRD INSTANCE OF ONE CLASS
1288: #  IN ONE ROUND. ***
1289: #
1290: #  The escaping mutant is a single operand swap using a value already present in
1291: #  the edited body -- squarely inside clause (d)'s enumeration:
1292: #
1293: #      store(A, DE + DRAWMODE, val=XFER-EN)   ->   val="0x03000000"
1294: #      (operator DEFANGED above: a verbatim copy of this store makes a mutator
1295: #       searching for it match THREE sites instead of two, and mine did.)
1296: #
1297: #  XFER is then never enabled, so `n25 rect' counts zero LINE messages and stays
1298: #  GREEN for the wrong reason -- and under M_NARROW_25 the folded rectangle
1299: #  reaches the LINE arm with XFER disabled, emits nothing, and THE INTENDED KILL
1300: #  IS DEFEATED.  All 38 rows green, BASE 38/38.
1301: #
1302: #  *** WHY MY EXISTING GUARD DID NOT COVER IT, in the seat's words: "the panel
1303: #  treated a positive control in a DIFFERENT SESSION as proof that the rectangle
1304: #  session did something.  It is not."  `n25 line' proves the LINE setup works in
1305: #  ITS session; `n25 rectliv' proves only that the trailing load ran.  Neither
1306: #  witnesses THIS session's stores.  My comment on the liveness row claimed
1307: #  otherwise and was false; it is corrected above. ***
1308: #
1309: #  This is the `xlatch all' failure for the THIRD time in one round -- a row green
1310: #  because its set-up silently did nothing -- and I had built the repair for the
1311: #  second instance INTO A DIFFERENT SESSION, where it cannot do the job.
1312: #
1313: #  THE WITNESS IS IN THE PRODUCT CODE ALREADY, and it was chosen by DUMPING the
1314: #  session's raw output BEFORE picking an oracle -- picking one after seeing which
1315: #  strings happen to be present is fitting the test to the binary.  draw_primitive
1316: #  announces its entry with BOTH operands this row needs:
1317: #
1318: #      [ sgi_de: STARTING DRAWING COMMAND: op = 0x03000000, drawmode=0x200000 ... ]
1319: #
1320: #  `op = 0x03000000' pins that the RECTANGLE store landed and the draw was
1321: #  entered; `drawmode=0x200000' pins that the XFER_EN store landed.  Under the
1322: #  swap the operand reads 0x3000000 and this row reds.  Under M_NARROW_25 the line
1323: #  is UNCHANGED -- the mask governs the switch, not the announcement -- so the
1324: #  witness does not mask the kill it exists to protect.
1325: #  *** AND THIS WITNESS WAS ITSELF GREEN FOR THE WRONG REASON.  Seventh pass,
1326: #  same class, and this time INSIDE THE REPAIR FOR ITS OWN PREVIOUS INSTANCE. ***
1327: #
1328: #  The oracle was `drawmode=0x200000' with nothing after it, and the device
1329: #  prints `drawmode=0x%x' -- so it matches INSIDE `drawmode=0x2000000'.  Swapping
1330: #  XFER_EN for the TRIANGLE constant 0x02000000 (a value this probe already
1331: #  stores in the pt/tri loop) leaves the witness SATISFIED while XFER is never
1332: #  enabled.  It only ever worked because the earlier seat's mutant used
1333: #  0x03000000, which happens not to prefix-match.
1334: #
1335: #  *** THIS IS A RE-RUN OF A FINDING I WAS GIVEN IN PASS 4 -- decl rows counting a
1336: #  substring shared with another string -- WHICH I FIXED THERE AND THEN
1337: #  REINTRODUCED HERE IN NEW CODE. ***
1338: #
1339: #  THE ANCHOR: the format is `drawmode=0x%x src_mode=0x%x', so a trailing
1340: #  ` src_mode=' TERMINATES the match and no wider value can satisfy it.
1341: row("n25 rectset", t.count("op = 0x03000000, drawmode=0x200000 src_mode="), 1,
1342:     "BOTH stores landed -- anchored so a WIDER drawmode cannot satisfy it")
1343: 
1344: #  *** MEASURED, NOT ASSERTED: negctl_n25_rectset.out in this round's artifacts.
1345: #  Under the seat's own swap the three older rows all stay GREEN and ONLY this
1346: #  one reds -- 38/39.  So the hole was real (without this row the mutant is a
1347: #  clean sweep) and the repair closes it, from the SAME run. ***
1348: #
1349: #  *** AND THE WITNESS IS STILL NOT SUFFICIENT IN KIND.  A second seat, asked
1350: #  whether the repair had the same defect one level down, answered: "the witness
1351: #  pins what the device was HANDED; it does not witness that the diagnostic is
1352: #  ARMED." ***
1353: #
1354: #  ITS ESCAPE, and it is a different shape from the conjunct already disclosed
1355: #  above: key the LINE arm's guard to op's VALUE --
1356: #
1357: #      if (drawmode & DE_DRAWMODE_XFER_EN && op == DE_PRIM_LINE)
1358: #
1359: #  which reads as harmless redundancy, since op IS LINE inside that arm.  The
1360: #  rectangle session's announcement is unchanged, so `n25 rectset' still reads 1;
1361: #  a misrouted RECTANGLE fails the added op-check, so `n25 rect' still reads 0;
1362: #  and `n25 line' fires normally because its op really is LINE.  **The whole pair
1363: #  passes with the misroute live.**  The seat named the structural reason: the two
1364: #  sessions differ ONLY in op's value, so ANY op-keyed guard splits them.
1365: #
1366: #  *** WHY NO FURTHER ROW CLOSES THIS, and it is worth stating rather than
1367: #  chasing: an in-session POSITIVE control for an ABSENCE is impossible when the
1368: #  control would require the absent event.  Session A is DEFINED by the LINE
1369: #  diagnostic not firing; nothing inside it can prove that diagnostic was armed,
1370: #  because proving it would mean firing it. ***
1371: #
1372: #  DISCLOSED, on the same scope rule as the conjunct: both escapes edit PRODUCT
1373: #  CODE this diff does not touch, so they lie outside clause (d)'s diff-closed
1374: #  enumeration -- coverage debt to state, not a floor breach to repair.  What
1375: #  WOULD close them is a channel that observes WHICH PRIMITIVE RAN rather than a
1376: #  diagnostic that happens to live in one arm; that is the same drawing-oracle
1377: #  round already filed as `sgideprimopdraw'.
1378: 
1379: A = Addr(CODE)
1380: t, alive, how = session(store(A, DE + DRAWMODE, val=XFER_EN)
1381:                         + store(A, DE + PRIM_START, val="0x01000000")
1382:                         + load(A, 0xffffffff80002000) + ["print v0"])
1383: row("n25 line", t.count(XFER_LINE), 1,
1384:     "...and the SAME channel DOES fire for LINE -- so `n25 rect' is not vacuous")
1385: 
1386: #  *** WHAT THIS PAIR DOES NOT CATCH, NAMED RATHER THAN LEFT UNKNOWN.  A review
1387: #  seat was asked for the smallest edit that defeats the pair and found one. ***
1388: #
1389: #  ONE ADDED CONJUNCT on the product-code guard defeats both rows:
1390: #
1391: #      if ((drawmode & DE_DRAWMODE_XFER_EN) && !(op & 0x02000000))
1392: #
1393: #  That silences the diagnostic for a FOLDED RECTANGLE while keeping it firing
1394: #  for a genuine LINE -- `n25 rect' 0, `n25 line' 1, both green, and the
1395: #  rectangle still drawn as a line.  The seat reached it by elimination and said
1396: #  why nothing smaller works: the two rows differ in exactly ONE input (the op
1397: #  value), so any edit leaving both green must make the string fire IFF the op
1398: #  is LINE-shaped, i.e. it must consult `op'.  It checked the single-token
1399: #  substitutions (drawmode->op, &->&&, &->|, & ~op, constant swaps) and every
1400: #  one either kills the channel for both ops or fires for both, reddening a row.
1401: #
1402: #  *** DISCLOSED, NOT REPAIRED, AND THE REASON IS A SCOPE RULE RATHER THAN
1403: #  DIFFICULTY: that guard is PRODUCT CODE THIS ROUND DOES NOT TOUCH.  Clause
1404: #  (d)'s enumeration is closed over the diff's own hunks -- predicates dominating
1405: #  the edited lines, plus operand swaps from fields the guarded body reads.  A
1406: #  conjunct added to an unrelated guard is a hypothetical future edit, which is
1407: #  COVERAGE DEBT to state, not a floor breach to repair.  Stating it is what
1408: #  makes a later escape "outside the contract" rather than an argument. ***
1409: #
1410: #  The seat's own summary is the honest one: the two-row SHAPE is right and the
1411: #  ORACLE is the weak point -- no arrangement of string-count rows closes this.
1412: #  Closing it needs a channel that observes WHICH PRIMITIVE RAN rather than a
1413: #  diagnostic that happens to sit in one arm.
1414: #
1415: #  AND ONE DEPENDENCY, because the same seat asked whether `n25 line' could go
1416: #  green for a reason other than the one it claims: it could, if a SECOND site
1417: #  emitted the same string for LINE-shaped ops.  Measured today --
1418: #  `grep -c "XFER_EN for LINE op"` over the device source returns ONE.  That is a
1419: #  layout fact, not an invariant: a future duplicate of that diagnostic silently
1420: #  unpins this row, in the same way a struct reorder can unpin an anchored row.
1421: 
1422: 
1423: # ------------------------------------------------------------------ summary
1424: #  *** A DELIBERATE NO-ROW MUST NOT RENDER THE SAME AS AN UNRUN ONE, and a review
1425: #  seat pointed out that a CONTRACT COMMENT does exactly that: nothing appears in
1426: #  the OUTPUT either way.  So the exclusions are PRINTED -- as notes, not as rows.
1427: #  They are deliberately NOT rows: a row that can never fail is the vacuity this
1428: #  file exists to refuse, and printing a note keeps the record visible in every run
1429: #  without pretending a test happened. ***
1430: print("NOROW  the drawing itself                       -- a declined op draws nothing"
1431:       " and no row asserts what a correct POINT or TRIANGLE would render")
1432: #  Pass 15: a seat pointed out the summary printed only the drawing exclusion while the
1433: #  contract had grown four measured latch-bypass limits.  A deliberate no-row must not
1434: #  render the same as an unrun one, and that applies to every limit the contract states.
1435: print("NOROW  latch bypass: equality to an unpinned value -- op == 0x07000000 MEASURED"
1436:       " full-score (table 12); infinite family, declared, not hunted")
1437: print("NOROW  latch bypass: operand sub-bit / threshold  -- fg & 0x80 MEASURED full-score"
1438:       " (table 12); infinite family, declared, not hunted")
1439: print("NOROW  latch bypass: bit relations / Boolean fns  -- (op&1) != ((op>>1)&1) predicted"
1440:       " full-score; concrete in-scope examples: nr_of_bits_in_the_middle != 1,"
1441:       " src_x != -1, saved_src_x != 0xffff (all-ones collides with the XFER-off"
1442:       " sentinel); every register/operand pins <= 2 values; declared")
1443: print("NOROW  latch bypass: cross-field compounds        -- op closure and latch-field"
1444:       " closure live in different sessions; declared")
1445: 
1446: bad = [n for n, ok in rows if not ok]
1447: print("OPUS500_RESULT=%d/%d" % (len(rows) - len(bad), len(rows)))
1448: print("OPUS500_LABEL=%s" % LABEL)
1449: if bad:
1450:     print("OPUS500_RED=%s" % ",".join(bad))
1451: print("OPUS500_DONE")

